      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>Large hats are still the order of the day. The fancy shapes are gradually ousting the sailor, but the latter has not by any means, altogether, disappeared. It really seems to have gained so much favour that it will ever be with us. The hats required now are those of very light texture, which are so comfortable on sunny days. They should have a wide brim to protect the eyes. Besides this advantage I think they always look becoming. Turbans of tulle are also very much worn. I have seen several hats lately of yellow straw, trimmed with black machine-stitch, which is certainly a novelty. There are also a few rice straw hats about visibly machined with coloured silk. Hats of a mixture of pink and pale blue are much in favour just now. Needless to say they are all trimmed with very light material. The crowns of some of the hats I have seen recently were covered with fine net—l have noticed them in different shades. Feathers are worn on the left side, whilst a locg one generally lies on the opposite bum. A very pretty ornament completes the hat. Hats are worn just about straight on the head, so you must make your coiffure accordingly.</p>
        <p>Frilled sunshades are used only for very dressy occasions, the plain ones being in fashion for every day wear. I think therl is nothing so pretty as a nice parasol trimmed with frills of chiffon. They axe very fashionable now in the West End and all the principal watering places. Of course, you have to recognise that a trimmed parasol is not intended so much for its usefulness as for its looks and therefore it is not everybody who would care to carry one. An important point to remember, however, if you do go in for one, is that the trim- mings of chiffon should match the dress. You eau realise how hideous a parasol of red would look of a slightly lighter colour than the dress a lady is wear- ing. Of course, on the other hand, there are many pleasing contrasts to be noted. I fancy it makes a person look complete and well dressed if the parasol is of the same colour as the person's dress. Smart parasols are profusely trimmed with lace. White silk sun- shades have appliqués of fine black lace on the outside and gauged linings of chiffon inside. Coloured silk parasols are trimmed outside with light ruehings of chiffon, and others are lined through with chiffon of a contrasting shade, which has a little puffing at the edge.</p>
        <p>Really the different styles of blouses are legion, and it a person cannot now be suited she must indeed be hard to please. At the same time, I must confess, I have seen many and many a blouse that looked quite abaurd by reason of its unsuit- ableness for the wearer. The other day I saw a most elaborate blouse, which a lady in my com- pany was wearing. It was of white muslin. The blouse was trimmed with pale blue and it had tucked front and revers trimmed with wavy in- sertions of fine embroidery, and edged with frills of lace. The collar was tucked and there were frills of lace at the throat and wrists and a cravat bow of muslin in front.</p>
        <p>I may here remark that the fashionable voile dresses have appliques of creamy lace on the shaped skirts, and also on the bodices and sleeves. These are usually bought in the form of robes, with the skirt ready-made, and pieces ready trimmed with lace to make the bodice. In many cases the voile is out away from underneath parts of the design, so that a lining of a contrasting shade may be seen through the transparent lace. I am sure you will agree that almost everyone has a favourite method of washing cotton materials, bus many have not learned the secret of washing flannel so it will not shrink. Use warp soft water, and dissolve enough soap in it to make a good suds. Wash through two water of the same temperature. Do not, twist between the hands, but pass them through a wringer that is not screwed down very tight. Hang them where a gentle breeze will blow through the garments so that they will dry quickly. It is important that muslins, ginghams, and cali- coes should be starched properly. Put the amount of dry starch required into the vessel used for that purpose, and pour enough cold water over it to, make a thin, smooth paste. Set it on the stove and pour boiling water in it, stirring all the time to keep it from being lumpy. Boil three minutes, and after it is taken frorn the stove, add a table- spoonful of paraffin to half a gallon of starch. This will keep the irons from sticking, and give the clothes a beautiful gloss. a esolve a little gum arabic in water, making it the consistence of mucilage, and keep a bottleful of the solution in the laundry-room. A tablespoonful of this stirred into a gallon of starch, will make the clothes stiffar, and cause them to retain their stiffness longer than they would without it. Flour starch is excellent for coloured clothes.</p>
        <p>Your loving</p>
        <p>JENNIE.</p>
