      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</head>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>The Queen is a great lover of flowers, and her private rooms take the decorators a considerable time each morning. Ockurletis blooms however are employed as much as possible, her Majesty having a great objectin to the air of a room I)6ra heavy with perfume. On a table close to her favourite seat is always a bouquet, arranged by ore of the children of Princess Henry of Battenberg. These flowers, as often as rot, are Irons the fields and hedgerows, the little folks having hunted high and low in the early morning for" grannie's favor- ites."</p>
        <p>The Princess of Wales is likewise devoted to flowers, but unlike her Mlesty, prefers choice hothouse speci- mem Her rooms are always full of palms, ferns and blooms, every vase or orrament that will hold them be- ing called into requisition. Princess Charles of Denmark closely resembles her mother in this direction and is , moreover a capital gariener herself. When quite small children the Princess of Wales allowed her daughters each a small piece of ground to tend themselves, and in which to grew their favourite flowers. Princess Charles proved a mostenthusia stic gardener and has never ceased to interest herself in this particular hobby. The Princess cf Wales is a most graceful dancer, and before the death of the Duke of Clarence, was devoted to this amusement. It is snore than seven years since the sad event took place, but Her Royal Highness has never once indulged iz.si her favourite pastime, save in the formal quadrille, to open the State Balls at Bucking- ham Palace. Music and photography may now be std to be the Princess's favourite hobbis. She plays—in additien to the harmonium and piano—with much skill , the zither, for which she has composed sime charming I airs. These productinr s have been privately published, and the booklet is a &amp;iightful souvenir of a delightful I personality. Town houses are goinz,, out of favour, a fact that is causing consternation among many owners of, and agents for London mansions. The season grows shorter year by year,ard numbers of people who prefer to spend but a few weeks in the Metropolis, find a hotel or flat more convenient, and a less costly and complicated affair. This season th. Marchioness of Hastings, Lidy Claude Hamilton, Lady Angela Forbes. and Lady Kings- ton are amongst those who have indulged in a pretty flat in Belgravia or Mayfair, and they have one and all expressed themselves highly satisfied with the idea. There are many historic houses in Lend, n that have ceased to belong to the families whose names they hear, Marlborough house for instatce. which it is romoured the young Duchess desires to buy btck for the family of which she is now a. member, is the home of the Pe ince and Princess of Wales. Cambridge House where the Duke of Cambridge lived, and where Palmerston also</p>
        <p>lived, is now the Naval ar,d Militiry Club. Chesterfield House is owned by Lord Burton. Bute House knows the Marquis of Brie no more, and Dudley HouEe is tenanted hy a South African millionaire. "I never recommend anyone a dressmaker," a well- known society women was heard to remark the other day, to a girl who wanted Ler new gowns tr, be aby - lately perfect, "and nether will you if you are wise. No one can afford to make gratuitous enemies, and there is no more certain way of doing so." The scciety woman was wise in her gencration. She knew that an _ _ .</p>
        <p>ill-hung skirt, or an ill-setting sleeve can brecd bitter resentment. The dreesmaker,cf course, ought to be the victim, but as human justice is appallingly uncertain,it is the one who recommends her, upon whom the real Onus falls. From the two facts, first that you recom- mended a dressmaker, secondly that that dressmaker turned out a badly built gown, a woman deduces, with hopeless want of logic, but unerring precision, the pro- position that you take a personal pleasure in seeing her lock a guy. That is always the beginning of the end of friendship, fee quently it is the end right away. The pieces into which the Society's cast off clothes find their way are many and varied, more variei possibly than in the days when their destiny was governed by the thrifty lady's maid. It is no uncommon thing for a society leader to eIl a particularly elaborate gown for a good round sum to a museum in want of a specimen of modern dress, and it is a well-known fact that two or three dresses which figured at a recent famous ball, were so disposed of. Many of the less expensive toilettes figure on the stage, actresses, especially those touring in the provincee,being extremely partial to society's luxurious robes. Then few Society women nowadays evince the slightest objection to pose —in the selling line—as " dealers." Through advertisements they term a kind of connection, and every now and then send out a written list cf things to be disposed of. And undoubtedly the plan works well, 1 both ways. The Society woman who does not dare to , be seen more than a few times in the same cat, hat or gown, gets acme of her money back, and the purchaser obtains a dainty fresh article at, generally. ccnsiderably below half price. In three or four instances lately, the clergyman offi- ciating at marriage ceremonies has been presented with the material of the wedding dress for conversion into altar cloths, and so forth. The idea has doubtless been borrowed from a custom in vogue in the Roman Celia- lic Church, in which a young bride frequently presents the officiating priest with the stuff of her marriage gown for the purpose of making vestments. To draw the lino between the tailor and the dress- maker is growing more and more difficult, for the latter has advanced by leaps and bounds in the manipulat'on of cloth, and the former now condescends to do much mere pettiness and detail. Many dressmakers now em- ploy ;alters to do fittings, and take the wok home, and in this way they show capital tailored mrdeis, and at a charge much less than the regular tailor with a grand shop and so on. The numbers of the Early Rising Association duly met at sunrise on Parliament Hill, on Midsummer Day. The band is not a very large one, and has not develoved into a vast army as the promoters fondly anticipated. Pereonaliy I have no prejudices upon the subject. People who get up with the sun and find that the</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JULY 7, 1899</p>
        <p>... R° CA r -</p>
        <p>method suits them may go on doing so, and those who fird that a later hour suits them better may follow their isanation. The latter division is usually the more amiable, and less tiresome to deal with. People who rise with the sun never fail to talk about it. and they also invariably have an unpleasant little habit of expect- ing everyone to be as energetic as themselves. Insufficient skirl) produces a lamentable condition of the nervoes system, and consequently sets up disease of both mind and body. In some houses the nursery is roused at a certain hour, and the little ones bundled into their baths forthwith. This is a mistake. One child may be found awake, and quit? ready for its tub, another sleeping as soundly as possible. The latter should he left to finish its sleep, and not roused, as is too often the case in nurseryland. The same rule too should obtain with grown-up folks, whenever feasible. some can do well on six or seven hours sl?,ep, others again, need eight or nine. Those women who make; a study of their nomplexion should retire early, as 130011 after ten as possible, and rise directly they wake. On no account should they permit themselves to be roused from their slumbers.</p>
        <p>" MADGE."</p>
      </div>
