      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held in the Council Chamber of the Town Hall, on Tuesday evii. Toe Mayor (Alderman Wrieit), presided, and there were also present, :--Aldermen Fairclough. Blacinurst,Councillors Baird, Duncan, Tinnion, Eleatm, Shaw Gr'eu, Peaccck, John Evans, James Evans, ShillcDch, Lee, Benn4t, Mmaki, Jolley, Smethurst, John Roberts, John Crosfield. with Mr. J. Lyon Whi.tle (town clerk), Mr. Stanley Wilson (d9puty town clerk), Mc. James Deaq (sanitary engineer),Mr.J. Faithuril (borough treasurer), Mr. J. Slit !liffe (assist.aut.borough survey)r).</p>
        <p>MILK-SELLING AND NEWSPAPER CRYING ON SUNDAYS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Watch Committee it was reolved that the Chief Constable take steps t suppress the sell- ing of milk after 10 a.m. ad the callinz ot necvspaners on Sundays. It was also resolved that he Chief Con- stable he authoriz-A ta engage a conveyance tor jurors to view bodies im all cases where the distance exceeds h3lf-a-mile.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR proposed the adoption of fly: minutes.</p>
        <p>COUnCiliOr IdEATON Seconded':</p>
        <p>Councillor BENNHTT said he had it represented to him that the hour fired with reference to the sale of milk on Sundays was rat hor early. There was no objection to the general principle, but if the hour were a little later it would be more convenient. The MAYOR said the Committee had no wish t haras3 the milk dealers unduly, but it was their firm decision that ten o'clock was late enough. They had had many con:plaibt 3 of late, and the law, the Town Clerk advised them, fixed the hour at nine. He thought they would agree that the Committee had been quite reasonable in the matter.</p>
        <p>Councillor JAMES EVANS observed that some of the milk dealers had approached him and he agreed with what Councillor Bonnett had Eaid. Be thought that if the milk dealers were givea a rurther half an hour they would be satlsfied, Councillor MONKS : Let them start hal!-an-hour earler.</p>
        <p>Courcillor HE ITON : The great difficulty is that peopl9 art, col up. . . .</p>
        <p>JAMES Eress : It is not the milk dealt:l*s' fault.</p>
        <p>ThR MAYOR : The Committee were firmly of opinion that ten o'clock was a reasonable hour. The minutes were confirmed. THE SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE TO • VISIT THE CLIO.</p>
        <p>At a meeting held on the 151 h June the Inspector re. ported that 35 Cases had been taken brf..)re the magis- trates, and fines inflicted in 22 cases,five cases dismissed, five withdrawn, two orders made, and one boy sent to the Clio. Fifty-four cases were brought before the com- mittee and de Alt with. It was resolved that the mem- bers of the committee visit the Ship Clio on the 20zli July. Councillor SHAW GREEN, in moving the confirmation of the minutes, said they would notice that their annual resolution came up again, and the time had corns when they proposed to visit their patients on the Clio. The MAYOR: I hope you will take your cheque book, and leave it there. (Laughter.) Councillor SHILLCOCK seconded the aCtoption of the minutes, and they were agreed to.</p>
        <p>MESSRS. MAGINN'S PREMISES AGAIN. At a meeting of the Markets Committee the application of Messrs. Magian Bros., to be allowed to use their new premises in Arpley-street for horse and cattle sales was again considerel and it was resolved ,"That such premises be appointed a p!ace for the sale of horses for a period of six months from this date" and "That in the opinion of this committee, it is expedient that a public place be forthwith provided for the sale of horses and cattle." Councillor LEE proposed the confirmation of the minutes. Councillor JOHN ROBERTS seconded. Councillor TINNioN said it would be within their recollection that he had brought this matter before the Council on several occisiocs, and he was under the im- pression a month ago that it had been dealt with, and in such a manner that he would not find it on the agenda again. He might have been under a misapprehension, but he certainly expected that the amendment carried last month dealt with the matter fitally, and that the opinion of the Council was that this was cot a desirable place for the sale of horses. He understood if net was carried it would prevent Messrs. Maginn Bros., or any- one else, using the place for the sale of horses or cattle. It was necessary to go over the ground again but he would like to say that in the opinion of the inhabitants it was not a desirable place. The neighbourhood was not a suitable one, and the sewerage, ventilation and suchlike wera anything but whatlwere desirable for a place like that which had been erected. He did not object to Warrington having a cattle market but he was hoping to learn that a proper provision had been made for the carrying on of a busi- ness of this kind. He did not think it could be carried on under satisfactory conditions on the premiss of Messrs. Maginn Bros. Councillor LEE : Before the amendment is seconded</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: Does any gentleman ,secend the amend- ment</p>
        <p>There was no seconder, and the amendment fell through. Councillor LEE explained that the committee was acting entirely on the advice of the Town Clerk. They ba,l no power to refuse the application until they had provided a public place for the sale of horses and cattle They would see that a sub-committee had been appoin- ted to look out for a site.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : I hope steps will soon be taken to pro- vide a proper and suitable place. . . .</p>
        <p>The minutes were then confirmed. GAS COMMITTEE.—TBE RESERVE FUND. SATISFACTORY RESULI S.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Gas Committee it was resolved that the sum of £l,OOO be carried to the reserve fund. The DEPIITY•MA.YOR moved the confirmation of the minutes, and allu&amp;d to the resolution that £l,OOO be carried t the reserve fund. He was glad to say that that fund was approachinc, very nearly £lO,OOO, and that when it did reach approaching amount the interest would go towards the reduction of the rates. (Heir, hear). CDUnCiliot BEATON seconded. COURCiIIOr SHAM GREEN congratulated the Gas De- partment upon having had such a successful year, and that in addition to what they had been able to give towards the rates they could act apart £l,OOO out of the profits to make up the £lO,OOO, the interest npmn which would be devoted to the aid of the rates. It would, no doubt, be wanted.</p>
        <p>Councillor Glum,: : But still it is a peculiar comment on the past proceedings of the Gas Committee.</p>
        <p>The motion was carried. RIVERS COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>_ . At a meeting of this committee the following resolution was Passed :—" That the River Surveyor's log book and his observations on the discharge of lime and other refuse from Corporation sewers or private drains be submitted to the committee at each meeting." The minutes were approved, on the motion of Coun- cillor HBATON, seconded by COLlDeillOr CIOSFIELD. THE FEATHERS' HOTEL TO BE RE-BUILT. At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee the following resolution was adopted :—"Tuat the Feathers Hotel, in Bridge-strert, be re-built, and that Messrs William and Seger Owen be instructed to prepare the necessary drawings of a new hotel at a cost not exceed- hag £2,000. Mr Ruddoek certified that the illuminating power of the gas for the month of May was 17.75 candle.." _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ The MAYOR moved, and Councillor HEATON seconded, the passing of the minutes, Councill3r TINNION alluded to the resolution with re- gard to the Feathrs Hotel, and asked if it would be re- built on the same site.</p>
        <p>The 5.11Y0R : As near7asjpossible. Councillor Tremou supposed this was really a com- mercial transaction, and the best thipg that could be done with the ratepayers' money. Toe licence was valued a short time ago at £3,000, and it had now dropped to £2,000. The value was, however, practi- cally nil in the market. If money was the sole obj&lt;ct in vie.' it would be as well—if there were no other advan- tages to be gained to the town than that of £ s. d.— they should do the best they could in the circumstances. Could not the hotel be rebuilt in some other part of the town. aa the licenses in Bridge-strew were in a crowded condition ; £2,000 would not he wisely spent in that neighbourhood. He did not object to the Corporation running a business of this kind, but he shtuld be sorry hr the .e who had to make it pay if itjwere rebuilt in BridgF-street. Councillor BENNETT wished to explain why he voted for the resolution of the committee. It would be foolish to disregard the financial aspect of the question, or look at it in a purely Quixotic light. Although they did not take extreme views on the temperance question he and others were in favour of reasonable and rational reform, and•by rebuilding the house they could make an ex- periment and be one of the first municipalities in the c ,untry to try one particular kind of temperance reform which had many advocates and was a hopeful solution of the drink question—the municipalization of the liquor traffic. A 9 to the questiou of rebuilding the hotel in Bridge-street, to which reference had been made by Councillor. Tinnion, he thought that from a temperance point of view they had bett,r build it there than any- wh.r. nice. If they were poivt! to a ell the hone and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
