      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JULY 7, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JULY 7, 1899.</p>
        <p>icense it would be better to build it in some Inc ality not Su well provided. In Bridge-street they would telikely to do less harm than anywhere else as there were so many facilities there for drinking. He supported the minute oa the condition that the house was eventually run by the Corporation. If it was proposed to sell it he might possibly take a different stand later on. The MAYOR said he agreed almost entirely with Cone- cillrr Tionion's views if the matter could be so arranged, and the huildinl could be put up in some more useful place. The matter had been thrashed out tame after time, and the Committee were almost Pam-Aro:au-Ay of opinion that the present suggestion was till only way of disprsiog of it. If the hotel was to be transferred to another place it would have to be done on the authority ot the magistratea. _ .</p>
        <p>Counidifor TINNION Quits so. The MAYOR : Ws have no feeling in the matter. Councillor TINNION : My pint is tat lilt is re-built in Bridge-,treet it cannot be built on the same site. The MAYOR : Partly on the same site.. =Councillor TINNioN : Would the magistertes be com- pelled to grant the license ? The MAYOR : ft; weuld be entirely in their discretion, baz I think it is such a reasonable rcqu,st that they could not refuse.</p>
        <p>Councillor TINNION : IS this the last opportunity we hll have r,f dealiwz with the matter. Will the plans be produced and the tuilding erected ? The MAYOR . The matter will come before the Coun- cih again when the plans have been prepared. They will have to have the approval of the Council, and then be submitted to the magistrates, s 3 that the matter is not absolutely disposed of to-night. Councillor Memo : These plans might not b 3 suitable for another situation.</p>
        <p>_ The MAYOR: 'Rey could be so prepared that there would be no difficulty. The minutes were then approved. THE CORPORATION BILL.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR then said: I am very pleased to announce to you that I have to-day received a telenam from the borough member, Mr. Pinpoint, informing me that the Corporation Bill has been read a third time. (Hear, hear.) The Corporation are indebted to Mr. Pierpoint for the valuable assist ince he has renderel in helping forward the Bill through the final stages in toe House. He has nut himself to no litte trouble in his efforts te assist the Corporation. Ha r, hear.) The Council then rose.</p>
        <p>WITH THE WARRINGTON DAY TRIPPER AT BLACKPOOL. [By .` QueLP N."-] Warrington was well representeu at Blackpool on Saturday. At almost every turn some familiar Warrington face met my gaze. I was one of the large number of people who journeyed from this town so notee for its ales, wire, soaps and leather, to breezy Blackpoel. In fact for the nonce I was a day tripper. Such being the case I had, of course, to be up with the lark or perhaps a little earlier' than that feathered songster in order to taste ta the full the delights of a day trip. My experience of the same leads me to the opinion that to speak of the day tripper is a misnomer. To be strictly accurate he ought to be known as the day and night 'ripper. He leaves his bed at 4 or 5 o'clock a.m. in order to be at the railway station by six, end it he is fortunate he may arrive home again by mid-night. A great deal depends upon the if and the humour of the railway com- pany. The Frenchman is credited with having told us that "the _English take their plea- sures sadly." I don't know how it is but it is always the Frenchman who exercises his critical faculties on the English. It is a Frenchman, of course, who is reported to have spoken of the English language as resembling a French horn when he heard a Londoner say he was going to catc'n the 2 to 2 to Tooting train ! But I am inclined to agree with the Frenchman about sadly taking our pleasures if he refers to the day tripper. Given a fine day the pleasures of the day tripper may about counter- balance the displeasurse, but if a wet day is ex- perienced, to speak of the day tripper as a 1 pleasurer-seeker is litetally correct. There is more seeking than pleasure however. For s3me time before six o'clock on Saturday morning the streets of Warrington presented an animated appearance and as everyone seemed t 3 be hurrying to the railway stations with their band- boxes and in some instances half-awakened young- sters, the uninitiated might be pardoned if he thought they were fleeing from some scourge- smitten town. It was a quarter past six when I arrived at the Central Station and found the plat- form a seething mass of human beings waiting more or less patiently for other excursion trains. Messrs. Richmond's special train with its freight of 700 human beings was almost at the point of departure, but as I was the fortunate possessor of a first-class ticket I had no difficulty in finding a seat. Five minutes later the engine gave a short, shrill whistle and we steamed slowly out of the station to the relief of the excursionists who were to follow by later trains. To travel to Blackpool via the Cheshire Lines railway as far as Aintree and silence by the Lancashire and Yorkshire line is a new' experience for Warrington trippers. The Lon- don and North Western Railway have previously enjoyed the monopoly but teie year the cempany had too many excursions in hand and this gave the other companies a look in. TO say the least of it the route taken on Saturday is a round-about one and two and a halt hours is rather a long time to be occupied in a run from Warrington to Blackpool. But the day tripper is a very patient in- dividual and gets accustomed to covering short distances in long periods of time. There is a free and easy air about him which is as noticeable as it is desirable. and what cares he so long as he arrives in Blackpool in time for break- fast? That is his first thought on arrival, and like the Scotchman's experience in London, he has scarcely sniffed the ozone of "the Brighton of the North "before "bang goes saxpence " and a t•ifle more. He easily becomes a victim to the refreeh- moot touts who are all anxious to supply the very best breakfast to be obtained in Blackpool if you will only step inside. The Clerk of the Weather was extremely kind to the Warrington tripper on Saturday and I am rather inclined to propose a hearty vote of thanks to him for his thoughtful consideration Those who remained at home experienced rain both in the morning and at night, whilst at Blackpool not a drop - feU until the homeward journey was commenced. The Blackpool tripper was therefore extremely fortunate, and had 'ample opportunity of seeing " the sights" without let or hindrance. And Blackpool, without doubt, caters for him in a manner unapproached by any other seaside resort in England or out of it. With its immense sea-frontage; its miles of promenade; its splendid piers and pavilions; its attractive places of refreshment, and its gorgeous palaces of amusement, Blackpool stands unrivalled. It is somewhat interesting to notice the different proce- dure amongst trippers in attacking the extensive bill of fare which Blackpool presents. Broadly speaking, they may be divided into two classes, one class endeavouring to crowd the whole of the sights and attractions into one day's programme, and the other not attempting to avail themselves of them in the least. The last-mentioned class are a pecu- liar set of individuals. They go on a kind of drink- sampling parade. Having sampled the drink at one licensed place a visit is paid to another and another and another and thus a diversty is given to what seems a monotonous tour. To add just a little variety a game of billiards is engaged in and their ideas of Blackpool are practically elreuin- scribed by beer and billiards, and become more and more hazy as the time approaches for the departure of the hemeward train. You will meet a representa- tive of this class in the railway carriage makiag an attempt to enliven the proceedings by a more or less successful endeavour to sing" Role Britannia" or' " Who killed cock-robin ?" beating time the while with the stem of his pipe and a gesture all his own. Quite a different kind of tripper is he who tries • to see everything, and perhaps unknowingly does a hard day's work. It is he who calls into requisi- tion the landaus and brakes and puts en a superior air as he loeks with condescension on she poor in- dividuals who are content to take a leisurely walk. Having made a general survey, the round of amuse- ments is commenced and completed without excep- tion. Then there are all the attractions on the sea shore to be iedulged in, including of course such minor matters as donkey-riding, sailing, bathing, and the like. The day tripper, it is said, comes for his yearly wash to Blackpool but I am not going to cast such an aspersion upon the habits of cleanli- ness of the true Warringtonian. I was told of an in- cident which occurred at the bathing places at Blackpool. The husband of a portly Lancashire woman had made up his mind to bathe and despite her fears for his safety and her entreaties to stay,he made his way to the bathing machines. When he entered the water it was apparent to all onlookers that. he was a capable swimmer and that there was as little likelihood of him drowning as of a live duck. He to3k to showing his ability as a diver and when he disappeared under water the arms of the portly lady were raised in fear and alarm. "He's gone this time" she shouted, as the diver stayed a little longer than usual under water, but in a , moment he rose to the surface again to her great 'relief. This little scene continued for some time to the amusement of the onlookers,and one of them afterwards proffered the anxious lady the follow- 1 ing advice: "Thalmust tak 'im tosHarrogate next year where there is no waster." As the long day draws nearer its close the tripper presents a tired and forlorn appearance. Dusty and tired of his tramps abroad the last hour or two is generally epent in the purchase of presents and it is surprising to see the amount of patronage given to Blackpool rock. Why rock should be made syrebolical of Blackpool is mysteri- ous, for it is just a little bit of real rock that Blackpool could do with to stop the continual en- croachments of the angry sea,especially near Uncle Tom's cabin. Fer at least half an hour before the time appointed for the commencement of the re- turn journey the platforms ere alive with trippers end the railway officials have a lively experience. It is not surprising if they do get a little confused and give incoherent answers to their numerous questioners. A porter on being asked whieh was the right train for a certain place pointed to two which were arranged on either side of the platform and replied that it was either one or the other! But I and two brother quills. whose conduct had been ex- emplary throughout the day, had no difficulty whatever in finding the right train. Punctually to time we left Blackpool and its gaieties behind and a three hours jog brought us to Warrington again. A portion of that time was pleasantly spent in a game of "tip it" which shows to what extent time must have hung on our hands. It wee 11.30 pm, before Warrington was reached, but so utterly tired out and so oblivious and unconcerned were the travellers as to any progress being made by the engine that it would not have surprised them if they had found themselves still in the vicinity of Preston- A railway journey possesses interest up to a certain point, but if that is once exceeded all interest becomes deadened, and the railway company are at liberty to prolong or shorten the journey as they think fit without hurting the sus- ceptibilities of the traveller. It was PO with the triopere on Saturday, but as " All's well that ends well" I am not disposed to grumble.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>IN A TIGHT SQUEEZE.</head>
        <p>IN A TIGHT SQUEEZE.</p>
        <p>When the coal gives out, sometimes the fires unthr steamships' boilers are kept going by burning the wood- work of the vessel. In a tight squeeze human ingenuity finds out many a helpful expedient. Necessity is often the mother of invention. People 10,,t in forests have subsisted on nuts and berries until rescued. Sailors in open boats have mitigated the agony of thirst by chewing bits of leather. Nature tries to be as liberal as she can, and almost any- thing can be made useful in a sharp emergnncv. But that is all you elm say. Even Grrenlanciers,who drink animal oil and eat polar bears (urd,ss the bears first eat them), do ketter on civilised food, when an Arctic expedition manages to spare them POMO. You may consult Nansen, Peary, Sm., on this had.</p>
        <p>We infer that no human being,carildo w(li on slops— such as milk,broth,soup,soda water, Scl.'aThe walls of a house are best made of brick or stone, and the people who live in the house must be built of solid mat9rials as</p>
        <p>Alackaday ! however. here we-frequently meet up with a difficulty. Whilst* bricks, 3tuck together[with mortar, my be put anywhere,: solid victuals cannot be used in a human body whenever you like. They slip into their places beeutifully in the caeo of Mr. Jones, for instance, while his ,neighbour Smith regards: them with an aversion that amounts tolherror ; needing them at the same time as a house nerds a roek foundation. The sorriest item in Mrs. Henvrorth's letter, it seems to me, is where she says, "For weeks and weeks to- gether I took no send food at all ; I lived on soda water, milk,broth,soup, and such things." After telling us that it is hardly necessary for her te add that she became too weak and feeble to go about. One may keep out of the grave on a diet of that sort, but be doesn't work and sing on it. This lady's story is short and plain.and may, perhaps, be instructive to a lot of us who are.in like case. "In the autumn of 1896," she goes on to say, '"I began to feel miserable and ailing. The energy and life seemed gone from me—nor could I tell what the matter was. My appetite forsook me, and aft n• eating what little I did take, I had much pain at the stomach and chest. "For weeks and weeks together I look noisolid food ; I lived on soda water, milk, broth, soup, and such things. For several weeks I was confined to my bad. e, A doctor attended me, but I felt none the better fot what he did, although, in,so saying, Ido not mean to cast any reflection upon him or on his profession. It stands to reason that no doctor can cure a disease unless he has the medicine that is suited to it. " W bile I was in this condition, with no hope of relief, I happened one day to get to talking with Mrs. Wright a good neighbour of mine. I told her how I felt and what had been done for me without any benefit. At this time I was so bad I could get only a trifle of sleep at night; and heavy sweats broke out over 111`7, rapidly taking away the small remnant of strength I had left. It looked as if I were going into a decline ; and to that, as everyone knows, there is apt to be,but one end. " Hearing all this, and seeing how disheartened and low-Tirited I was, Mrs. Wright said she believed she knew a medicine that would help me if it did not cure me. She said she referred to Mother Seigel's Syrup. which had cured her when she was well nigh as bad as I Was.</p>
        <p>"On th's I got a bottle and began with it imme- diat:ly. In a few days I felt rOieved and allowed my- self to hope I would get well. Ling the syrup faith- fully, as directed, I was soon convinced that it would cure me. I commenced to eat stronger food and it gave me no trouble. Gradually I came to be able to eat good, solid meals—taking for a time a dose of the Syrup right away after eating—until I was in perfect health, and as activr and vigcrous as ever. "From that time on I had no return of the ailment, I can eat anything I want, and it does me good. I believe no one will ever have indigestion it he only takes an occasional dose of Mother Seigel's Syrup."—(Signed) (Mrs.) SAMUEL HENWORTII, 123, Elmore Green Road, near Walsall, &amp;uth Staffs., April 28, 1898.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>SAD CASE OF SUICIDE AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>A SAD CASE OF SUICIDE AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A sad case of suicide occurred on Friday evening last week, when John Hewitt, aged 31 years, of 39, Bridge- water-street. Warrington, committed suicide by hang- ing. It appears that the deceased had been ailing for the last three months, hut had only relinquishes follow- ing his employment during the last three weeks, when a severe attack of influenza had left him somewhat de- pressed. He witnessed the procession of the scholars in the early part of the morning, end returned home in le,tter spirits than usuai. During the course of the evening his wife had cccaeion to call him, and hearing no response she weut upstairs, and was horrified to find him suspended from a hook in one of the bedrooms. Much sympathy is felt for the be- reaved widow and her two little children.— The inquest on the body was held in the Parade Room at the Police Station on Menday afternoon tetore Mr Jones, deputy coroner.—Ann Eiewitt, widow, said her husband had complained lately of pains in his chest, back and head, and he had been attended by Dr. Mc- C4rthY• On Friday she was out with him and he drank three bot'les of stout. He had a nap in the after noon, and while 'en was asleep one of the claildren cried and rile gave him a slap on his face because she was afraid he would wake his" daddy." When the deceased came downstairs he inquired" What did you hit him for," and she replied" Because ho was naughty." The de- ceased then said " You should not have struck him," and premised the lad a ride in the" puffer" in the morning. She subsequently saw her 'husband cutting some rope, but her suspicions were not aroused, as she thought he was preparing something to attach to his fiehing rod. Betemen six and seven o'clock he went up- stairs, where he was found later on with the rope round his neck. The end had been fastened to a hook in the wall. She screamed and ran downstairs. She was too horrified to cut the rope. He appeared to be dead. One of the neighbours went upstairs and cut him down. In answer to the Chief Constable, witness said her hue- bind was not exactly sober when he carne in. He had never to her knowledge expressed in intention to com- mit suicide.—Jehn Thomas Burnham, a neighbour, tde- posed o cutting the rope. The bode of the deceased was quite warm,and they tried to restore animation but unsuccessfully. When the doctor came be said the man had been dead Some time. The man's feet were abcut 18 inches from the ground.---The jury returned a ver- dict to the effect that the deceased committed suicide while of unsound mind.</p>
      </div>
