      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>SCENE AT WARAINGION CENELitIL S'TATLON.</head>
        <p>SCENE AT WARAINGION CENELitIL S'TATLON.</p>
        <p>TRESPASSER OR PASSENGER 2-1 • NICE QUES HON.</p>
        <p>A very interesting c tie was la 11)hdar at the %VrringoH Borpugh Police Cour!:, bef ,re the ilayor in the chair Land five other rnagistrat•.s. Erasmus Barnham, a well dressed man living at Aidl Flous-lsne, Croft, was charged with having on Saturday night wilfully trei- passed on the Ch9shire Lae; Railway at the Central Station, Wtrrington, with havinl refused to quit when requester-I ti do so, and with havilg a Asaulted Herbert Gadimore,relirf stationmarer,and P.C. J.lllO McQaillarn, officer of the convpittee. G illirnezT was charged witn an assault on Barnham.--11r. Browne appeared for the defendant Barntram.</p>
        <p>Superintendent Simpson, who coeducted the case on behalf of the Committee, said that on Saturday night, shortly after ten o'clock, one of the cavities break 3 down and caused a block at the statiort. When the read had been cleared the trains camcs in se fast that the pas- sengers could not get off the platform quick enough and a eleetk was caused on the steps. The deferelant Barn- ham came up the steps at the time, and he stoed with his bock against the handrail, thus depriving females and children from a necessary support. He was asked by one of the officials to remove to the other side but he refused and the matter was reperced to Glliim are, who touched the man on the cap and asked him to "Stand on the other side." He use filthy longuage and refused to do so and at length the local police had be called and the defendant was forcibly removed into the street. Seortly afterwards, however. he returned and spoke to Mr. Davies, the booking clerk, and asked where the man was who had kicked him. He also asked for the stationmaster, and when Mr. Gellimore was pointed out to him he said " Are you the stationmastn ?'' and he r:‘eirived a reply in the affirmative. Na further conver- satim took place, aed the defendant was seen to de- liberately strike - Mr. Gallimore a violent blow on the face, causing him to stagger backwards. Fortuneely for hirri he gathered himself up, or he might have crime into contact with a tr in which just then entered toe platform. Barnham was then taken into custody. Herbert W. Grillimore, relief clerk, and station-master, was then called, and said be was in charge of the station on Saturday night. Tee defendant cam) to him and ask)d him if he was the stationmaster, and when he replied that he was Bemire, n strut him a severe blow in the face and knocked him backwaris. fled he not been a strong man he might have been knocked under the train.—By Mr. Browne: fie understood that the defendant lived at Croft,but he did not k9OW that at the time. There was now no train to Padgate on Satur- day night after 9.35. On the previous night there was a train after eleven o'clock but it had been stopped. The defendant was standing with his hack to the stonework. He did not throw the defendant's cap to the bottom of the steps, nor did he kick him on his fiaeers ; he was surprised that such a charge had been made against him. The man was not, to his knowledge, held on the floor, but he was taken to the waiting-room. Barnham was trespassing, because he was not a passenger. Teen was no train to the place he wished to go t. He was told so.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne: Ti I go to the platform and inquire about a train am I a trespasser? Witness : We allow passengers to go on the platform if they conduct themselves in a proper manner.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne : But he came Co sae whether there was a triia to Padgate, that being the neirest station to Cr,ft.</p>
        <p>Witneis : He had no occmion to go to the platform, as he was told there was no trAia.</p>
        <p>Joseph Wilson D svies,chief booking clerk, said he was ascending the steps o i the nightin question when he met a large numb.r of people coming off the platform. The result was that ho was unahl- to get up, and he stood on the Wiewick side, so that the pAssengers cou!d use the hand rail. He noticed the prisoner on the steps leaning against the opposite rail. He requested him to move, but he took no notice of him, and Mr. Gdlimore's atten- tonw is called to him. Mr. Gallimere touched his cap, and as tio defendant raised his he-td rather quickly the cap Eel off, and he turned to the stationmaster and used bad language. He was ejected by the police, but re- turned and asked for the man who hscl kicked him. He was directed to Mr. Gallimore, but he said that as not the stationmaster and he callel witness A liar. Defend- ant asked Mr. Galli more " \Vint right had you to kick me ?" and, without anoTher worl, he stru3k the com- plainant a violent blow in the face.—By Mr. Browne: Witness did not attempt to get up the steps whil . the crush was on, because he had mire sense. The defend- ant had no right on the steps, because he was not a pas- senger.</p>
        <p>John McQeillam, a policeman stationed at the Central Sottion, (mid he was also assaulted by Barnhana.—By Mr Browne: Witness did not kick Barnharn. Mr Browne for the defence, said the defeadaat had been a pensioner over 20 years, and he spent most of his time in making bowling greens and in gardening. On Saturdly night he wont to Warrington to draw his pension' and at ten o'clock he went to the Central Station to catch a 'bus for home, but:found that it was full. He then proceeded up the steps of the Central S atien in search of a train, but was met by a rush of people coming off the platform. He could not be re- garded as a tbraspasser ; belore he could be so regardei he must be there against the will of the Committee. The beoking office was in the centre of the platform and every passenger had a perfect right to go to it. The rusk was so great as he was going up the steps that he had to bold by the stone work to prevent himself being pushed away. It was then that Mr G allimore appeared on the scene and threw the deFendant's cap down the stepe--.a most annoying thing to do—and else kicked him on his fingers and (Impelled him to 10039. A Mr. Fazikerley, a friend of Barnham's, was on the steps at the time, and he would be able to tell the B3nch what teek place. The roan went back to the station for an explanation of the assault, as he had a perfect right to de. He denied that Mr. G ,Dimon was the stationmest r, lac also, he knew that that wag a position occupied by Mr. D trbystire. He certainly did strike Genially) in the face, but there was no mark left. Barnham was one against six or seven officials of the company, and the Bench ceuld, therefore, imagine what he would get compared to what he could give; them facts entirely disp aged of the chary, of a serious assault having been committed. Mr. Gellinsore had never said a word abeut the man being a trespasser until the cross-examination, and then it dawned upen him that the company bad ,no right to put him away. If Gallimore bad not thrown the cap down there would have been no disturbance, but he did an unjustifiable act. The defenlent Barnharn was locked up in the Warrington police cells, but he was bailed out on Sunday, having been in cus- tody 12 hours. Be (Mr. Browne) did not contend that his client had a right to commit an assault, but he was driven to it by what had occurred. He contended that Gallimore ought also to be convicted. Fazekerley said he also went to the 'bus at the Central Station, but found it full. He went up the station steps with the defendant, and he also saw a cap throws' down them, and Mr. Gallimore deliberately kick B arnham's hand off the steno coping.</p>
        <p>_ Defendant was also called and gave evielmce as to the alleged assault upon him. He said to Fazaaerley at the- tDiok-what he his done—he has kicked tile." He admitted striking Gallimpre. Ile was afterwards "run in to some office and knocked on the fl)3r."—By Mr. Simpson: The injury to his hand was not caused by a scythe; it was the injury to the other hand that had been so caused.</p>
        <p>The Bench then retired to consider their decision, and on their return in five or six minutes, The Mayor said: This case has bsen very carefully considered by tho Bench, and ths maj irity9.emed tri think that Bsraham hts acted in a vary persistent man- ner indeei. The least we can do in the circurnstanc-s is to fine him 204 and cost. The other case (against Gallimore) will be dismissed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>SUMMER, AND ITS PERILS.</head>
        <p>SUMMER, AND ITS PERILS.</p>
        <p>THE heat of summer, and its mild, relaxing weather, are often more dangerous 0 childno and adults than is commonly supposed. Profuse purspiration and the use of cold or effervescing drinks impoverish the blood, and the patimt is, as it were, starved. If any natural weak- ness exists, the system will break down at that point ; or a general relaxation and languor may set in almost unnoticed.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Christopher, of Sleaford House, Hitchin Hill, Hitchin, told the following story to a reporter who called upon her the. other day. The subject of the eon- vereation was her daughter, Mabel Christspher, agei nine years. Up to last summer this little girl had at- tended school regularly, but about July last year she lost power in her limbs, and on day they gave way so that she fell down in the street. She was put to bed,and there she remained for many weeks, for the use of her legs was soen entirely lost. She became gradually thin and pale, and was paralysed all over. Her mother, however, had read in the papers of the good which had been effected in cases of paralysis by the use of Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people, and she determined to try them. Mabel was still getting worse when the use of the pills was begun last August. Before she had taken the first box—a hex lasted a geed while, as she only had half a pill at a time—she had got over the worst, and gradually but surely began to mend. Tome or four boxes of pills in all were taken, and it was in November when the little patient first got out of doors after her long Ulla ,ss. " It was generally thought," said Mrs. Christopher, "that she would never get well. She could not bear to keep her legs straight, and I thought at first it was rheumatism. Her blood also was entirely out of order. She has not taken any medicine besides these pills, and now she is as well as ever she was, and is going to school as usual ; she does not look lake the same girl." This is an instance where an actual illness was caused by summer heat ; in other cases as we have said, there may only be a general deb!lity an i d signs of running down. A cure, or a safeguard which s le,tter, will be found in Dr. Willi ms pink pills for pale people—the pills with Dr. Williams' name on the wrapper, not a Bubsti- tut2—and the same remely may be coafidently recom- mended for anmmia, weakness, St. Vitus' dance, rheumatism, paralysis, and consumption. If substitutes are offered, the genuine pills may always l-e obtained of Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, Holborn Viaduct, Len- don—price two shillings and ninepence, postage free.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <p>ITiRRINGTON TRAINING COLLEGE.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL PRIZE DlsrmurioN</p>
        <p>The annual ;),'IZ3 distrinution Co th 3 successful students at the Warrington Training Collrga was held on Saturday, tie Dean or Cnester presiding. Thera were also on the platform: the Rev M )rloy S:evanson (Priqci- pal), the Rev Canon Willis (Rector), Dr. Watkins and Mr S E Haworth. A large compsay of relatives, friends and otners interested, harl assembled to witness the proceedings, despite the unpromising state of the we tther. inclu ling the following :—Rev E C E anti Mrs Carleton, Rev. H. V. aid Mrs. Figo7,, Rev. E. and Mrs T.:shuck, R iv H A and Mrs Lster, Rev F S and Mrs P.:well, Rev V/ R and Mrs Hartwright, Rev E and Mrs Wathered, Rev and Hoe C F Cross, Rev G B Thurs- ton, Rev J Carson, Dr and Mrs Guest G)rnall, Dr and Mrs Sherratt, Dr and Miss B &gt;wdan, Mrs Beamunt, Miss Webb, Mrs Shirn, Mrs Stevenson, Mrs Willis, Captain. Mrs and Miss Withiu.c,rton. Mr and Mrs Edward Woods, Mrs and Miss Fairclsulli, Mrs and Miss Cozen, Mr and Mrs KcGregor, Mr and Mrs Maria, Mr and Mrs E i win Wools, Mr Mrs and Mist May Mr and Mrs Turner, Mrs Paarse,and Miss Pierpoint,Miss Lax, the Misses Watkins. the Iffisres Comber, Mr and Mrs Nickson, Mr and Mrs Bas wick, Miss Warmingten, Mr Carruthers, Mr E M Hance, Mr 0 Duthie, Mr Danby, Dirr Wainwright, Miss Kenyon, Miss Ingham, Miss M l'irnew4l, Miss Cole, and Miss Anderson. Of the resid- ent staff of the College there were present the Misses Fenitnan, Timewell, Perry, Bell, Brooke, Ilackett,.and Gordon. .</p>
        <p>The Rev. MORLEY STE7ENSIN said he did not propose to intervene mere than a few minute; between them and toe Dean of Olegel. It was rigatshe thought, that on an occasion such as this the Principil should give some account of the work and progress of the college during the past year. It was not, however, an easy task as the results of most of their examinations had not yet been received and indeed their fin-al examinatieu would only take place the following week: Whetever the result might he he was sure he might bespeak from them ou behalf of these young people their ' best wishes. There were many pleasures belongine to youth which were denied to them in lacer life, and one of them was examinations. But, if he could tell them little or nettling of the results of examinations he could tell them something of inspections. Those of them who had to do with schools knew that next to examinations the greate4 treat afforded to them was inspections. A training college was very like a school in a constant state of multiplication. Where the school had one examination, the college had six : where the echool had one inspection the nonage had three. In one respect he tru=ted there was a similarity, between them. He could truthfully say it would be im- possible for them to have pleasanter or fairer inspectors titan they had. He was glad to take that opportunity of publicly etatine what he was sure his brother principals would cordiahy, enclone, that the visits of their inspec- tors were indeed a real pleasure, that by their kindness and cluitesy to staff and students alike they make tor themselves a ready welcome and leave behind them a pleasant memory. They hid first of all the Archbishops' Inspector, tee Rev. Prebendary Rey- nolds. Ho came from the two Archbishops to look into the spiritual welfare and the religious instruction of the College. In his repor: he said " I Can only speak in terms of the highest praise of your College. I cannot even make a eagge,tion that would produce anything better than what you have already. The College im- presses me in a marked degree as having above all things the spirituel welfare of the students as its first ()eject an yet doing the other work with equal success. The students evidsntly enter into the spirt of the Col- lage. The work of such a Colleee as yours cannot fail of lasting results in many schools." Thea they had a visit from the Government Inspector, Mr. Scott Coward, whs spent some days with them and looked very thoroughly into every:hing. He spoke every highly of their premises as sanitary, satisfactory, well adorned and dignified. He spoke of the staff as loyal, harmonious, diligent and working wite good results. The reading and recitation he pro- nounced to be ueifonnly good and carefully taught.' The lessons which he heard the students give showed aim, seriousness and sympathy with the class, lie praised the Cllustrati ins which had been prepared by the students and remarked Heat as a general rosult a high average of pract-cal suectss was reached. Finally, he said that the whole attitude of the colleee was true : the students were taught to look upon the work as a dignified one and that there was a general concensus of opmien as to the excellence of Warringtoa. The Hon. Mrs. Colborne, directress of needlework, after hearing every second year student give a lesson in needlework, reported "an average set of students, conscientioes workers,but not brilliant teachers. There was great uni- formity in the quality of the test exercises, welch were carried out most satisfactorily. There work of the junior studeats was satislactory bath in qaantityand quality." Lastly, Sir Jahn Stainer said : "It is impossible to speak too highly of the musical work of this college, not the least praiseworthy part of it being the care and attention bestowed on students who entered in a back- ward st ice. No branch ef work showed weakntss. A series of charming songs were sung with good taste. The students of bath years were supplemented by male voluateers, and a string quartet for the choral perform- ance,:and a most meritorious and enj ey able rendering was given of Mendslssohn's psalm "As the heart pants" and his cantata "Laud() Sion.' He (Mr. Stevenson) thought they would agree with him that these reports covering among them almost tee whole field of their work were as satisfactory as they could wish, and were speaking witnesses fee the efficaeacy of the College. He had now discharged the duty of giving them some account of their progress and work, and would proceed to the more pleasant duty cf asking the Dean of Chester to address them all, and ti distribute the prizes to the fortunate winners. The Dean was not only an old friend of this College, and a long standing member of its Council, but having held the office of Archbishops' Inspector ot Training Colleges for many years, he had an intimate acquaintance with the work of their Col- leges, an acquaintance which would enable him to speak with the knowledge of an expert, and which would demand from them all, that attention for his words , which he felt sure they would receive. • (Applause). The Mete, who was received with applause, said that nothing could be more gratifying t 3 them than to beer the report of the very high level which • had been at- tained. He was sure tbat all would understand the high level was not due to the committee but to the Principal. (Applause.) Ho was equally sure the Principal would tell them that the committee were willing to do all that lay in their power,not only to help him in the work,but also to help him in making it as agreeable as possible. Tney had it under their eareest consiieration to'exsenl the size of the college, that more students migat enter and partake of the benefits from the discipline and in- structien they would receive. Ho trusted it would be made known far and wide amongst -their-friends-that it was desirable to extend the college. It was no use to, hide facts from themselves ; they were in the face of educational difficulties. The whole of the coubtry was in one mind that religion should not be driven out of the elemsntary schools. (Applause.) There were con- scientious people who were of the opinion that educa- tion shouid be secular, but the great majority desire that their chiloren should in taught relieions truths. He thought they would agree with him that the work of education could not in perfectly and truly done if there was to be no religious education, no ippettl to the conscience. (Hear, hear.) He had a list before him which showed that it was well done there. The Princi- pal had referred to the visa he paid 19 years ago to that and other institutions, and in that colleee,in which he was so especially interested, he was able to rejoice at the advancement of the students in the religioas ataidies. Some 60 students were going east of the college shortly to take up more or less difficult positions, and he would I appeal to them to regard their duties as the noblest of work. There were great warnino to be taken from the state of opinion in France at the present time. What was the bottom of tne immorality of the trial the country was labouring under ? It was a want of truth, justice, and fairness. It sprang partly indeed from political causes. but more so front the banishment cl religious teaching from the selaools. In this* country the vast majority were in tavour of the retention of religious education as part of the curriculum of their colleges and schools. They must not be blind to the fact that there was a minority which did not desire it. It was said that without religious education there would h 4 a greater advancement and development, but he thought not. The speaker procee- ded to refer to one of the disqualifications he was Moan- ing under. He said that 28 years ago he attended a meetaig at Leeds, and the subjects under discussion was whether elementary masers should be made inspectoie. One Lancashire gentleman strosgly obj.cted to such a procedure, because elementary masters knew too much. (Laughter). Perhaps he (the speaker), having, bean an inspector, knew too much, for when he came to give away those prizes he thought of the pressure. The prin- cipal said they wore judged by results. Certainly they would fairly judge the recipients by their results. They had also been told that examinations were pending on which much depended. He wished tivin the very highest SOCCAFS. If there were any who were disap- pointed in the actual results, in not having secured a prize, he would Liee to ;rant ow the real result was t be found in the culture of the mind. (Hear, hear.) His nee did not bth eein when he joined e interest committeein thein Ii 1877. He had been interested in the college since the year 1844. His interest in the place was of very old standing, 60 old, tha-, perheps it, might be brought to a close, but he was not quite so sure of that: There was one thing he ceuld-promise them, and that was, as long as he was in cite neighhouring dioseie he would never forget them. (Loud applaus)).</p>
        <p>The Rev. CANON WILLIS moved a vote of 'thanks to the Dran for presiding. Dr. WATKINS seconded the pronosition. He said hA had had the privilege ef wo.klog with the Dean f3r 18 years, but he thought that when he was the secretary he must have been a trouble to the Dean. It was a great pleasure to him to be presaat, and ho must say (turning Ow ads the 4tll lent q) how blooming, he slthy, and happy they look )(I, (Laughter.) They must not run away with the idea of Jvnoing into hp,d.mistringli positions at once. The best thing for a girl was to be an assistant in a large school rather than a head-oaistrEss in a small oue. (Hra., he tr )</p>
      </div>
