      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>WATER POLO.</head>
        <p>WATER POLO.</p>
        <p>LIVERPOOL AND DISTRICT WATER POLO AS-zOCIATION. PRESENTATION OF TEIE SHIELD. At the conclusion of the water polo matches at the St. Helens Baths, on Tuesday night, Hr. F. D. Nevin, ia the absence of both the prosident of the association and the president of the St. Helens Swimming Club (Coun- cillor F. R. Dion-Nutta.li), presented the shield n f the ass.)ciation to Cant tin Ripley, on behalf of the St. Helene Club, who last year won the championelip. The shield, whichii of massive silver gilt, is mounted on another shield of blue silk plush, sal bears the coat of arms of Liverpool, and a sketch of a water polo match in progress. It bears the in3cription--'• Liverpool and Di4trict Water Polo Association. Barker Memorial Shield 1896." On it there are a number of minor shields,upen which the names of winners will be inscribed. The names already on are St. Helens, 1836; Evert on, 1897 ; and St. Helens, 1898."</p>
        <p>• ST. HELENS v. SEFf LIVERPOOL AND DISTRICIs LEAGUE. A FARCICAL GAME. At the Corporation Baths,St. Fielens,on Tuesday night a large crowd witnessed the rkturn League match bs- tween St. Heleos v Sefton, the first match having re suited in a 4 *Ails to nil victory for St. Helens. The following players entend the water at 8-35 under the direction of Mr F D Nevin. Liverpool :—Si Helens : J Aspinwall, goal; J Brettoo and T Green, backs ; P Ripley, half-back; W Preston J Mavity, and J Lawren- son forwards. Setton : P Preston, goal; M Parry and Bradshaw, full backs; M Jones, half-back; H Sprat, T Bowen, and J Ard. forwards. Defending the deep end first, Sefton opened well, and Jones defeated Mavity for p)esession. From a font, howeTer,Lawren- son received trom BrAten, and scored. Bradshawve- calved and shot, hut A!,pin wall saved. and Green,receiv- itig, swam down and gave to Preston who shot wide. Mavity put the ball through immediately after, but the point was disallowed. After Lawrenson and Ripley had each shied at the goal, Lsverenson scored a second. The same player had one disallowed. He. however, made up for it by swimming down with the ball, and Pagelle over to Mavity in the nick of time enabled him to score the third amid claecrs. _lmmediately after,Mavity scored a fourn goal. St Helens kept up the pressure after this; while Sefton 1t them do as they liked without any exer- tion. Ripley banged a fifth and 8;h goal through before the interval. Half time St Helens, 6 goals ; Sefton,. all.</p>
        <p>In the second half, Ripley continually sent down low shots, several of which Pragnall saved. One, however, he tailed to hold, and it riflel through. The Sefton cus-odian in saving a tremendous shot received injury, but continued toe game. Lawrenson again scored, while Preston afror feigning to shoot, which the goal- keePer attempted to save, rat:led a couple through straight off the reel. The Sefton men made ne attempts to play tho game and Rip'ey scored the eleventh goal, after which the vferea curtailed the match, which bad. developed into a complete farce. The score of II goals to nil is a record for the Sr. Helens team io League eon- tssts. Final result : S. Helens, II goals ; Sefton, nil. •</p>
        <p>Sr. HELENS No y. LIVERPOOL ST. GEORGE'S. LIVERPOOL AND DISTRICT COMBINATION. The first meeting of these teams took pla.co at St. Helens on Tuesday night, before a goo 1 attendance. S. Helens defended the deep end first, and the champions of the third division last year made a good show in the initial half of the game. Play was most evenly con- tested, each side attacking in turn. Close on the half- time Stannitord scored for St. George', who led at the idterval by 1 goal to none. On resuming Sz Helens put a diff.?rent complexion on the game, and Walker equa- lised, and put hi i side ahead immediately after. He again scored, while Burrows scored a fourth. The homesters continued to do as they liked, and goals were added by Wgiker (2) and Naylor (2). Final result : St. Helens 2nd 8 goals, Liverpool St. George's 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
