      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>EXAMINEI.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINEI.</p>
        <p>JULY 7th 1899.</p>
        <p>THE Licensing Reports deal Aeduction, Compensa- with these three points,and tion, and Prohibition. henceforth the controversy Will rage around them. Sunday closing, the re- Atriction of bond-fide travellers to hotels and restaurants and the fixing of a seven miles' limit, the licensing of the brewer only in oases of tied houses, and the suggestions made as to licensing Magistrates, drunkenness, and the registration of clubs may be regarded as minor and less contro- versial matters. The majority report concedes that a large reduction of licensed houses is required. The minority report also gives it prominence, and furnishes us with a scheme, which, of course,raises the question of compensation. It is curious that the two reports agree in fixing seven years as the Period for termination and compensation. Indeed the majority favour the plan of granting all new licenses for seven years, when they are to be ter- Minable. The minority think that the licensing authority should determine how many licenses arO required in its district, and then give all holders seven years' notice. The reduction may commence at once—at the end of the first year—and the holder of any license to be taken away would then receive: seven times the rateable value of his premises. The reduction might go on bit by bit every year, the compensa- tion being always on the basis of the years re- quired to make up the seven. A loan would have to be raised on the license rentals unaffected by the scheme, and licensing duties would, of course, be increased all round. The majority report, which is supposed to represent the views of " the trade," would find the money for compensation by an annual levy of 6s. Bd. per cent. on the capital value of ordinary licensed premises, hotels and restaurants paying one-sixteenth of their rateable value, with a sum from clubs and new licenses. That is, if the majority and the minority agree in three things—a reduction, a seven years' licensing period in substitution for the annual license, and Compensation by an additional license duty. It is aftething to have established these three points 48 a basis for legislation, unless the Licensing Commission is to be regarded as specially alined at by Lord SALISBURY'S fling at Royal Commissions that would come to nothing. When we come to the third point we also find More agreement than might have been expected. The majority say that they are satisfied there is no general desire for prohibition by plebiscite. The Majority's conclusion is that "we have no evid- ence before us that public opinion in England, Whatever it may be in Scotland and Wales, is at all strong enough to justify such a measure " ; and Ile prohibition could not be tried until the seven •7eare' period of reduction had passed, they add: ." It might be as well to postpone any decision as .t° its adoption or otherwise until that period of transition has expired." This does not seem to us to be an extreme view. It is sensible, and will eceive strong support. Of course, if compensa- tion is to be as settled a point as it is in the judg- Ment of the majority and the minority, local pro hibition hardly becomes a ;practical policy, and it Will have to be given up. This is the conclusion to which public opinion is likely to come, and though it may seem to be a re.;rogression, on the other hand it may be said that no considerably Populous district in any part of the country is Sufficiently advanced to try the Maine Law, with all its absurdities in working.</p>
        <p>THE whole civilised world has Captainllre-fw been moved by the pathos of the return of the victim of a gang of French _forgers and military traitors. From his solitary 141E1 Captain Dreyfus has returned, a prematurely :ged man, to be tried over again for an offence we 11°%v that he did not commit. Acquittal may ,Dr°lsent problems almost as grave as a second:ver- fet of guilty. The popular mind has been in- 'lamed with that worst of all passions—religious fanaticism. To many Captain Dreyfus is still the clever traitor, the betrayer of his country, the de- sigtling and unpatriotic Jew. The Government Ordered the finding of the Court of Cassation to be P,G,sted up in all the communes, so that the public k"eich does not read newspapers might learn what '41:1 been done and what was going on. But how could it be expected that one official act would wipe '3llt of the minds of the ignorant the series of calum- Idliee Which have been hurled at DREYFUS any time ,11,..rf0g the last two years? Even at Rennes, ere the new court-martial is to be held, and i'aPtain DREYFUS is in prison, the ignorant popu- ace are still at the stage of exasperation which l'as Common in Paris when M. ZoLA began his ?a4lPaign. The fear is that when the second trial s over and Captain DE,EYrns is acquitted there 51.1 still be danger from the madness of the people 41,11 d the false patriotism of some soldiers of the ePublic. The French clovernment which has cell strong enough to bring him back to France,</p>
        <p>must carefully consider the responsibility which the position will enforce upon it. The personal safety of Captain DREYFUS must be as precious to the French Government as his aersonal vindication, and when the trial is over at Rennes, which may not be for several weeks, it must see that no harm comes to him, or the whole civilised world will cry shame upon it. It is here where we fear most for the victim of four years' exile and outrage. There are desperadoes who do not mind executing an act of private vengeance, and there are soldiers in France who might imagine they were doing a deed that would make the army safe from spies and traitors hereafter. Until the public mind can be restored to sanity, and the aberrations due to the action of the military officers who have conspired against DREYFUS have been corrected by facts and national declarations, there will be some danger of assassination. The point must be considered. Vindicated and restored to his rank and position, is there any chance of Captain DREYFUS living a safe and useful life in the French army? We are inclined to doubt it. He troy not himself desire it. What the Govern- ment should do would be to give him pecuniary compensation for his sufferings, and to find him some post where he could still serve the State. If this be impossible or imprudent, he should be allowed to retire from the army with honour, if he cannot remain in it with safety and find in it a successful career. He might then find a refuge where he would not cease to be a patriot, and might not be the mark of hatred and revenge.</p>
        <p>It must have been an amusing sight to witness the exodus of that host of tatterdemalion Russian spies which took place from Finland recently. One of the ingenious devices adopted by the Czar to secure the Russianisation of the Finns without undue strife was to stir up class rtgainst class, pro- mising the poor and ignorant doles of land and financial assistance if they would oppose the Anti-Russian policy of Finnish people who are higher up in the social scale. To carry out this holy plan an army of spies was organised in St. Petersburg. The spies were sent into Finland in the disguise of pedlars, and their mission consisted in doing all they could to stir up the rural popula- tion. They were to extol the beneficence and generosity of a Czar who had broken his corona- tion oath, and to paint with a broad brush the iniquities of those Finns who objected to coming more directly under the sway of his great and good Uovernment. When the pe Ears actually started work, however, the Finland Municipal Councils soon devised a remedy. Each commune offered £2 reward for every pedlar caught in the act, and every person who sheltered a spy had to pay a heavy fine, the money thus collected going to a local fund "for the extermination of vermin." These measures have proved effective. The other day three hundred pedlars left for St. Petersburg in a body. Further batches are expected to leave as soon as funds are supplied to them for railway travelling. They will all go to the Czar's capital to demand their solace money. A more ridiculous climax in a shameful conspiracy has not happened within recent times, but unfortunately there is no reason to believe that it will affect the Czar's determination.</p>
        <p>It is some satisfaction to find that public opinion is moving many of the principal trusts which operate in America and Europe to formu- late a defence to the charges made against them by consumers. Hitherto the trusts have attempted no defence, but have overcome opponents by sheer weight cf money power. Now they are looking out for more or less expert debaters to defend them at company meetings and in the press. A favourite device of all these people is to trace the history of industrial combination, pointing alike to its harmlessness and its needfulness, but there is no attempt to deal with the later developments of trade autocracy, which condemn the small trader to ruin, or such subordination as deprives him of the ability to rise, and in some cases com- pel the consumer to face death in the interests of trust sharemongers. The oil monopoly is a case in point. It is no defence to say that it has cheapened oil and assisted in the prosperity of hundreds of affiliated trades. That will not con- done the deaths of the unhappy people who have suffered from low-flash oil. It will not comfort the independent trader who has had to go down before vindictive competition. Huge cm porations nearly always show a tendency to oppression. In numerous cases they cannot help it—it is one of the laws of their existence. In other cases they deliberately pursue an oppressive policy for profit- able purposes. These trusts are in a state of bar- barous savagery, and until they become civilised the warfare of public opinion cannot be expected to cease. Even then serious economic and social problems arising out of them make it a moot point whether they are sufficiently beneficial to justify existence.</p>
        <p>A lamentable case was unfolded at the Coroner's Inquiry held on Monday in the Police 'Station at Warrington respecting a woman who had died from excessive drinking. She had cele- brated Walking Day like many another one in Warrington by imbibing too freely. Unable to walk she fell down in the yard and was conveyed to an outhouse by neighbours where she was found dead by her husband three hours afterwards. A distressing feature of the case is that she had only been married three weeks. Her husband had known her for five years previously and was aware that she was addicted to drink at the time he married her. The Coroner's advice to him was to marry a sober woman next time. This is rather cold comfort for a man whose wife still lies unburied, and even if he followed the advice it might possibly lead to the same dis- astrous results. There are numbers of men who have married sober women but who in later years have broken down before the temptations which continually assail them. Perhaps it would have been better under the circumstances if the Coroner had shown more respect for the feelings of the bereaved man, who told the Court with tears in his eyes that he "thought a great deal of her," if he had kept his advice for a more favourable opportunity.</p>
        <p>Scenes such as that which occurred at the meet- ing of the Birkenhead Town Council on Wednes- day are fortunately rare in the history of our municipal authorities; in fact, we do not recollect another instance of certain members of a Town Council being called to account for bad behaviour by their brother members. In this case the protest of the minority against the majority seems to have been justified by the proceedings said to have taken place on the trial trip of the ferry steamer Claughton. Members of the Corporation are charged with drunkenness—one is sufficiently honest and repentant to have publicly apologised—and altogether there can be no doubt that the career of the Claughton was commenced amid scenes of insobriety and disorderliness of a particularly disgraceful character for a Corpora- tion to be implicated in. At Wednesday's meeting one Councillor moved a -iesolution proh;.biting the expenditure of ratepayers' money on intoxicating liquors for any public function, This resolution was not passed, the majority of the councillors objecting to any such restriction on their privileges in the future, hut by deserting the meeting and avoiding a vote they practically admitted the justice of the censure proposed to be laid upon them. Public opinion in Birkenhead has been raised to a high pitch by the lurid accounts of the Claughton adair and the developments at the Coun- cil meeting have not tended to restore the confid- ence of the people in their representatives. To be a member of the Birkenhead Corporation cannot be a very enviable distinction at the present time.</p>
        <p>James Cartwright's system of living, as detailed in the Warrington Police Court last week may suit James Cartwright but it is not approved of by the Guardians. This man is siLgle, earning regu- lar wages, and might be a useful townsman if he</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>aabti</head>
        <p>aabti</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY JULY 7, 1899.</p>
        <p>had not an overpowering affection for strong drink. Of late he has been on the spree and having succeeded in drinking himself into a state of " D.T," he finally landed in the Workhouse hospital, and became chargeable upon the War- rington Union. Now the Guardians are not averse to doing their best for a man, but when that man wilfully neglects himself and falls back upon them for medical attention and sustenance they naturally feel that they have a title to redress, especially if their patient is well able to support himself financially. Thus matters fell out in James Cartwright's case, and the Guar- dians decided to invoke the aid of the magistrates in order to bring him to a propef sense of the fit- ness of things. The magistrates convicted Cart- wright on a charge of wilfully neglecting to main- tain himself, and he had to pay ss. and costs. If other Unions will only take a similar action in such cases as these, we shall hear and see less of men who drink, drink, drink till they lose all sensibility and are then content to be taken charge of by the Guardians, and maintained at the expense of the ratepayers.</p>
        <p>Throughout Warrington district there appears to be an epidemic of scarlet fever of a mild type. The Warrington hospital is full, and the ac- modacomtion for the nursing staff is taxed to the utmost. The question of further hospital exten- sion will have to be seriously considered by the Health Committee at an early date. Too much importance cannot be attached to the value of isolation far infectious diseases, and with the rapid and continued growth of the town further accommodation is much needed at the hospital in Aikin-street. Warrington is, however, much better served in this respect than Stockton Heath where isolation is impossible owing to the lack of a hospital, and where disinfection only seems to have been attended to in a somewhat perfunctory manner, owing, it ts alleged, to some parents wilfully re- fusing to call in a doctor. Such action is a menace to the whole neighbourhood, and if it has been carried on to any great extent we are not surprised ifthe report that 70 or 80 cases have occurred is correct.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>RUNCORN RURA.L DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURA.L DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of this Council was held at Dutton Workhouse, on Tuesday. Mr. J. Knowles presided,and there were also present :—MeEsra J Mosley Williams, S Woodward, T Wright, J B Chadwick, C E Linaker, W Piling, J Mclntyre. W Bretherton, T Lewis, P Leather, W Davenport, T Darlington, W Fletcher. with Mr. George Ashton (clerk), Mr W E Hour.h (assistant clerk), Mr J Farrington (inspector), and Messrs W Diggle and J Swinton (surveyors), and Dr Adams (medi- cal officer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>HELSBY BEWERAGE.-TENDING INQUIRY.</head>
        <p>HELSBY BEWERAGE.-TENDING INQUIRY.</p>
        <p>- At a meeting ot the Helsby Parish Council, held on Monday, 3rd July. on the proposition of Mr. Collier, seconded hy Mr. Whittle, it was unanimously resolved to adopt Mr. ttadclige's scheme for works of sewerage. and that it be a recommendation to the District Council that application be made to the Local Government Bard for sanction to the scheme, and the holding of the re- quisite Local Government Board inquiry.—lt was de- cided tut Mr. Diegle's salary be increased by £l2 for the year, ending 21st March, in respect of extra remu- neration. and that the Local Government Board be asked to sanction it.—The Nuisance Inspector's and Surveyors reports were also considered. Mr. Linaker, as one representing a considerable inter- est in the township, thought the scheme was an extra- vagant one, and ought not to be carried out. He did not see why treatment by gravitation should not suffice for Heleby as it did at Frodsham. The rateable value of the township was £B,OOO, and he thought to spend £5,000 on a sewerage scheme was most extravagant, and he strongly objected to it. The Inspector pointed out that at the Local Govern- ment inquiry each ratepayer would have the privilege of being heard. Mr. Linaker said he had good reason to believe that the majority of those who voted for the inquiry did so under the impression that they would get expert opinion from the Local Government Board's Inspector at a cheap rate, but that was a mistake. The minutes were coafirmet nat:row's AFFAIRS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Halton Parochial Committee held on the 24th June, Mr. Thomaa Wright presiding, it was resolved that a vote of condolence be accorded to the family of the late clerk in their bereavement. The gum- tion of granting extra remuneration to Mr. Diggle for his services in preparing plane for a eewerage scheme at Halton Brook was considered and the Clerk explained that the queetion had been considered by the Distriot Council and they had referred it to the Parochial Com- mittee. After a full discussion it was resolved on the motion of •Mr. Looker, aec)nded by Mr. Mannering that in consideration of the work already performed by Mr. Diggle in the parish of Halton it be a recommendation to the District Council that for the twelve months ending the 31st day of March 1900, Mr. Diggle'a salary be increased by £l2 10s and that application be made to the Local Government Board for in order declaring such increase cf salary chargeable as special expenses upon the township of Halton. It was also req)lved that when the sewerage scheme for Halton Brook is carried out the question of further remuneration be eoneidered.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr. T. Wright seconded by Mr. J. B. Chaiwick the minut.s were adopted.</p>
        <p>TILE LATE CLERK'S REMUNERATION.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Hoard wrote ganctioning the additional remuneration of the late clerk (Colonel Ash- ton) for services: as Parochial Clerk in the several parishes as under :—Frodsham, £25; Frodsham Lord- shin £l5; Haltan, £10; Helsby, £l5; Stockton Heath, £25 : Sutton. MO.</p>
        <p>TIIE ISOL&amp;TION EtOSPITA.L.•••-REPORT OF PROGRESS.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrote adverting to the letter dated 13•11 February, received from the Runeern District Council, with reference to the provision of hos- pital accommodation for the isolation of oases of infec- tious diseases occurring in the Rural District, and en- quiring what had since been done in the matter. On the motion el Mr. J. Mosley Willianisjt wag me_ animouslv resolved that the clerk reply to the following effect " That the hospital Committee had bad several sites under consideration for the erection of suitable premises, hut in every instance such difficulties hid been encounted as to put a stop to their proceedine with their building and for the present the matter remains under con ideratinn." WALTON INFERIOR PARISH COUNCIL ON ITS DIONITY. Mr. .1. Mounfield, clerk to the Walton Inferior Psrish Council wrote at the direetion of the Parish Council stating they had examined the pipes recently laid down in the old brook, and were thcrottehly dissatisfied with the job. As the tide rose in the river the water came through the pipes and filled the old brook hed,and when it receded. the water found its way back through the many holes in the joints of the pipes conveying with it the mod and soil, so that in a very short time the pipes would be filled up and useless. There was also a drain from Brooke Cottage net connected at all and the sewer- age runs about the pipes and lies in the old brook bed. With respect to the road beyond the archea at the corner ef Hey Lane it was three years in IJuly since the District Council's attention had been called to it. The road was dangerous ani the brook in an unprotected tonditinn. Any acci- dents happening there the District Council would be re- sponsible for. He had been Instrusted to lay the matter before the County Council as the Pariah Council was de- termined to be free fr sm all responsibility. Mr. Swinton, in reply to the Chairman, said that the work tenon the brook had not been completed. The Clerk was instructed to reply aceordingly. SCARLET FEVIIR AT STOCKTON HEATH, Mr. Farrington (Inspector of Nuisances) reported upon ' the outbreak of scarlet fever at Stockton Heash as followe :—" I find the : total number of premises infected and alleged to be infected to he 22. and the offietoctearl onf uHmebaletrh, al cases to be 29, each of which (with one exception) have been visited by the Medical must have been duly notifi7dns. eqintewntalys T2OB of such port.sdt ocamse: that 2 cases were nnt fever, but as to the correctness of ' this statement I cannot give any pronf. I was informed thet the occupier of one haute returned to me as being infected, hal removed into the Borough of Warrington , if this has been done without having the vacated nre- , mieee previously disinfected, such oecupier is lieble under " The Infectious (Prevention) Act. 1890," to a penalty not exceeline £lO.-12 premises are net as vet ready for disinfection ; 4 have been disinfected by the occupiers in a satisfactory manner. and 3 rooms have been fumigated and the bedding removed therefrom and put through the steam disinfector at the cost of the Council.—From informs- tion received there is not the slightest doubt but there havel been eases in which no Medical man has been called in, although I failed to nbtain any direct proof of this. In such cases,the t,ue nature of the , disease is not known, and no precautions are taken to prevent it enreading. and the proviaiena of the " Infectious Disease (Notification) Act, 1889." are set at naught, thus giving the disease every opportunity of obtaining a firm hold of the community.—ln conclusion I may again explain that in my opinion scarlet sever is not a " Filth dieease, but is generally spread by infection, and a common medium by which infection in cenveyed is by the foolish practice of healthy P'rgong visiting infect3d premises without taking duo precau- tions, and at presant there is 11, provision contained in any of the Acts of Parliarn nit relatini, to the Ivalth of the public, wher,hy such p-actics can be sunnvss'd. I have circulated a quantity of posters containing information respecting infeetioue diseases', as di reeted at the last meeting, copies of which I now submit,"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>SHORTHAND SUCCESS.</head>
        <p>A SHORTHAND SUCCESS.</p>
        <p>We are pleased to announce that Mr. Thomas Ciar? was successful at the recent examination in connection with the National Federation of Shorthand Writers' Associations, and has been awarded a certificate for a five minutes' test at the rate of 120 words per minute. Mr. Clare is a member of the Runcorn S.W. A. DISASTROUS STACK FIRE NEAR WIDNES.</p>
        <p>About half-past one o'clock yesterday morning, five Widnes cyclists returning home discovered that four stacks and some loose straw at Bennett's Farm, Spek e, were on fire. They at once raised an alarm, and the Liverpool and three other fire brigades were sent for. Every effort was made to extinguish the flames. The -damage done amounted to £5OO or £6OO-</p>
        <p>BRUNSWICK SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVER.SARY SERVICES.</p>
        <p>This Sunday School. which has will been described as " Runcorn's Cradle of Nonconformity " holds its anniversary next Sunday. The preacher will be the Rev. E. Theodore Carrier, of Liverpool. On Monday in .;onnection with the same cause the Rev. J. T. Wardle Stafford will deliver his popular lecture " Shall we go back to Rome ?" (An appeal to the Protestants). The chairman will be Mr James Dedds,of Lymm. The lecture should prove more than usually attractive ; especially in these times of rottenness in the inner parts of the estab- lished Church. At the service on Sunday special music will be sung by the St. Paul's choir and Sunday School scholars.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED THEFT FROAL A FLAT AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Before the Runcorn Bench, on Wednesday morning, Samuel Ellis, senior, Stringers-court, Runcorn, and Samuel Ellis, junior, his son,were charged with stealing the sum of £ll from the flat " Fanny "lying at the Runcorn Docks, betwem the hours of 2 p.m. on Monday. and 6 a.m. on Tuesday.—No evidence was given against the younger prisoner and he was discharged.—The older prisoner was remanded until 11 a.m. on Friday.—Frorn the evidence submitted it would appear that John Littler was the captain of the flat. About six o'clock on Tuesday morning he missed £ll from a shelf in the cabin. The money was safe enough at 2 o'clock the previous day. He left the boat on Monday about 4 o'clock but did not return until 10 o'clock the same night, and the following morning he missed the money. Ellis had been in his employ for about six weeks as mate. Upon the police receiving information of the theft Super- intendent Pearson, in company with Constable Carring- ton investigated the case, which resulted in the arrest of the prisoners.—The case is somewhat complicated, and some interesting evidence may be expected on Friday.</p>
        <p>RESULT OF THE SCIENCE AND ART ESAMINATIONS</p>
        <p>The following are the results to date of the recent Science and Artexaminatiens held at the Runcorn Tech- nical Institute :—Physiography, Elementary, let class : —Annie M Barrow, Ada Hill, Hannah Moors, Harry Turton. 2nd Class :—Daisy Robinson. Advanced, 2nd class :—Margaret Harrison. Machine Construction and drawing, Elementary, Ist class :—Joseph C Hewitt, Wallworth, Baguley, Joseph E Worrall, Charles H Timmins, William M Armstrong, Thomas G Deakin, John E Tweddell, Samuel Clarke Richard L Fryer. 2nd class :—George H Powell, James Aspy. Adyancel. Ist Class:—Walter H Wiswall,Joha W Wiswall 2nd class:—Rowland Hadfield,Frederick Kilcross William G Cragie, John A Rawlinson. Honours. Part 1, pass : Percy T Williams. Building Construction, Elementary, ISt Class J Harrop, Thomas P Mort, James Armstrong, Thomas N Withington, Robert W Booth, Frederick Guy, William Allan, Marcus Mellor. 2nd class :—Thomas Hill. Hanry Hill. Leo. C Dixon, Arthur Cropper, John W. Sandbach. Thomas E Farras Advanced, 2nd class :—Charles E Bradshaw, William B Cropper, Arthur F. Midge. George Pennington. Sydney Maddock. Mathematics, let stage, let class, Josf.ph H Bankes, Emily Simkin. 2nd class : William Woods, Teresa Barry, Elsie Cameron. ST. MARY'S CHURCH, WIDNES. _ . _ _</p>
        <p>The annual field day in connection with the Sunday school of the above church was held on Saturday after- noon. 0 wing to the unsettled state of the weather tea was partaken of in the Waterloo-road schools, after which the scholars, to the number of about 700. marched in processional order to Mr. Randles' field in Deacon's- road, headed by Gossage's band, which played lively airs and selections en route. On arrival at the field the youngsters quickly separatd, and were soon disporting themselves to their hearts' content on the grass. All kinds of amusements were indulged in until the hour of departure. The procession was then reformed as before, and a move was made for the schools. The weather was unpropitious for such an event, but notwithstand- ing the adverse circumstances a greac deal of pleasure was derived from the few hours that were spent in the field. On Monday the Vicar, Rev. C. E. Stocks. M.A., departed from Widnes for a well-earned holiday- His immediate journey was to Manchester, but on Wednes- day he will continue his tour in Switzerland with the hope of reaching his destination, the Canton Vallais. on Thursday night. Hero he will take up the chaplaincy of Saint Nicholasmear Zermatt, for four weeks. ACCIDENT TO A WIDNES EXCURSIONIST.</p>
        <p>An accident occurred to a member of the excurion of the Widnes Corporation emp10765 to Blackpool last Thursday, which somewhat marred the pl.asure of a section of the trippers. During the day John Jones, aged 42, foreman of the nigh'soil department, and raid- ing at 14, Major Cross-street, whilst goinv down the slope to the shore slinped on the stones and fell, fractur- ing his ribs. His injuries - ere attended to, and he was conveyed 'to the station,and on arrival at Widnes he was immediately removed to the Accident Hospital. Sr.</p>
        <p>JOHN AMBULANCE _ASSOCIATION-.RUNCIRN BRANCH.</p>
        <p>The examination of the second and third year student; of the above-named association, took place on Wednes- day last week at the Technical Institute. Dr. MeLennon, of Runcorn was the examiner, and submitted the students t 3 a lengthy test of their theoretical and practical knowledge of first aid work, with the result gratifying to everyone concerned, that all succeeded in attaining the rNuisit standard. After the close of the examination, Dr. Mc Lerman addressed a few erar,eful and appropriate words to the students on the utility of the ambulance work, and advised them not to allow the knowledem acquired to slip from them. He expressed himself pleased with the manner in which they had met his interrogations that evening. Mr. J. H. Weston proposed a vote of thanks to Dr. McLennon for his courteous and patient examination, which was seconded by Mr. W. Shaw. The proposition was acceded t. by all present with great cordiality. The folio wine are the names of the succes-- ful student; :—J H Weston, H Doug.las, F W Hutton, G Lydiate, W Cooke, T Tonks, H Moores, H Marsh, J Mack, B Trevett, W Jones. A Wmodwa.rd, J Worrell, W Shaw, F K Welsh, and Police-sonstables Smith and Upton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES,</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES,</p>
        <p>BY A CORRESPONDENT.' The 99th Anniversary of the Brunswick Schools will take place on Sunday, when the annual pro- ceedings of assembling the scholars in the capacious gallery will be observed. In connection with the Anniversary the Rev. J. T. Wardle Stafford will deliver a lecture on " Shall we go back to Rome ?" This will be on the Monday night, and if the oppor- tunity of hearin7 Mr. Stafford is lost, some people will have to reproach themselves for missing a good thing, RS Mr. Stafford is credited with being a remarkable lecturer.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Board of Guardians are troubled with a knotty question. It is how to treat a cer- tain incorrigible inmate.—All kinds of treatment they say have been tried: save one, I now add ad- visedly. That one a certain medical gentleman apnears to have somewhere "up his sleeve," and I believe that the Guardians are thinking of entering into negotiations with that "medico," and are anxious that he should take the '• incorrigible "in hand for say three months.</p>
        <p>Tc is intended to connect the Workhouse at Dut- ton with civilisation by the introduction of the telephone. The project is to connect the Ntedical Officer's house, the offices of the Clerk, and the Police Station the cost of which is estimated at £27. The idea is a good one and commends itself, but it must not be forgotten that the whole hangs upon the supposition of the establishment of a centre at Dutton.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Wiswall (thanks to Dr. McDougall) is now placed before the public in an entirely new light ; and receives the honour which has been due to him for some considerable time. On Wednesday, Mr. Wiswall, in his official capacity a, chairman of the Urban District C)un^.ll,"entertained the' members o the Council and other influential gentlemen to a trip up the Manchester Ship Canal to Manchester (Salford Dock', returning by way of the Bridge- water Canal. The outing was enjoyable to he body and educational to the mind: and illustrated the amicable spirit that prevails between the Urban Dis- trict. Council and the Manchester Ship Canal Com- pany. Runcorn should gather courage from the statements made by Mr. Oldfield " that the benefits of the Manchester Ship Canal Company must eventu- ally benefit Runcorn and its advent as an im- portant city "maw not be many years remote. A detailed report of the proceedings appears in another column.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn School Board, for the purpose of aranting labour certificates, will hold an examina- tion at Victoria-rd. Board Schools on Tuesday next, the 11th inst. Parents wishing to avail themselves ,of this great advantage should see that the names of their children are given in at once to the teachers of the schools the pupils attend, or to Mr. J. W. Lightburn (the clEr; of the School Board), who has very kindly intimated that he will give all the assistance and information possible to parents and scholars, if applied to at once. J. BAYLEY IsAtc offPrs SPECIAL TERMS to SCHOOLS for his flepiendid PORK PINS for Walking Day.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>NARROW ESCAPR FROM DROWSINO.</p>
        <p>John Lewis Dodd, aged 7, 110,0akland-3treet,Widnes. on Saturday whilst playing on the wall by the promen- ade which abuts the Mersey, slipped and fell into the river. Assistance was quickly forthcoming, and he was speedily hauled out and conveyed to the Accident Hos- pital, where he was detained for a couple of days. A RUNAWAY HORSE AT WIDNES,</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening, a horse and trap, which had arrived in Widnes from Skelrnersdale, was being driven along Waterloo-road when the horse suddenly took fright and bolted. As a consequence of the mad rush of the horse the trap was smashed in two, but fortunately no further damage was done, the occupants escaping unhurt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>RUNCORN TRADING. SHIP LOST.</head>
        <p>A RUNCORN TRADING. SHIP LOST.</p>
        <p>Captain James Wilson, of Runcorn, and his crew of three men were cast ashore at Lytham on Sunday. and reported that the coasting steamer Peru with gravel from Fleetwood to Liverpool had foundered eleven miles out. Captain Wilson found that the vessel was making water, and he set three pumps to work, but these had little effect and the water continued to gain groutad,and the fires being put out, the craw abandoned hr. She sank in seven fathoms of water twenty minutes later, and the men had a long pull in the small boat to Lytham. ANNIVERSARY SERVICES AT LACEY-STREET,</p>
        <p>The anniversary services of the Lacey-street Wraleyan Sunday School. Widnes, were held on Sunlay. The pul- pit was occupied morning and evening by the Rev. Albert Clayton, London, secretary of the Twentieth Century Fund, who preached to excellent sermons to large congregations. In the afternoon Mr. R. Cadwalla- der Itaby gave an address to the scholars, taking for his subject " The Prodigal Son." Special hymns and music were rendered ty the children and choir, which had been admirably trained by Mr. E Evans. Mr. W. Stan- ley presided at the organ at the morning and evening services. and Master Hague and Miss Swindells officia- ted in the afternoon. The collections amounted to over £lO2, £BO of which had been subscribed by the scholars, forming a record contribution for the Sunday School. RUNCORN INSTITUTE SCHOOL</p>
        <p>One of the pupils at present attending the above school. George Clement Baguley, son of Mr. C. Baguley, Manor Farm, Norton, has bmn succe,sful in gaining one of the Cheshire County Council's Agricultural scholar- ships. These scholarships, tenable at the County Coun- cil's Agricultural School at Holmes Chapel, are of the value of £3O a year and may be held for or is, two, or three years. The course of instruction includes practi- cal work in farming and gardening together with scien- tific instruction. No one who fails to get 70 per cent, of the total number of marks attainable is eligible for a scholarship. We have now several county scholars in attendance at the Institute who have come from Ele- mentary Schools, but Bagulev is the first boy from the achool who has had the honour of gaining one of the hieher County Scholarships tenable at a place of higher education.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>MERSEY MISSION TO SAILORS.</head>
        <p>MERSEY MISSION TO SAILORS.</p>
        <p>The first open-air concert, in connection with the Mersey Mission to Seamen, was given en Saturday after- noon by the Volunteer Band, on the Sailors' Recreatien Field, adjnining the Holloway. The band was ably con- duced by Mr. Outhwaite. The ungettled state of the weather no doubt told against the attendance, hut the rain ceased during the concert, land the band SubmittPd an excellent programme, which seemed to be highly appreciated. The swings and the merry-go-rounds were then a great source of atraction to the children, while tbe adults found recreation in feo`ball and other amuse- ments. A further item was a race for youtha which excited considerable Interest. The venture proved eminently satisfactory, and the sailors, boat people and chi'dren who partook in it will no doubt CAP that their friends get to know of the eff)rts made to entertain them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>MILTON_ P.S. A., WIDNES.</head>
        <p>MILTON_ P.S. A., WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The annual distribution of priz9s in connection with this society took place on Sunday afternoon, and a foir attendance was presided over by Mr. J. Harvey. Two solos were rendered in an effective manner by Miss Hill, of Runcorn. The address was given by Mr W. J. Stainer, who gave corns canit.l advice on bloks. He based his'remarks on Bacoa's advice to readers, ',There are books to be tasted, and books to be swallowed, and books to be chewed and digested." He said that in tasting a book it should be read and then put on one side. There were other br.olcs to be ewlllowed which meant that they should be read fairly, and the hooks to be chewed and digested de,ervel to be re Id carefully and fully, and copious notes to be taken of them. To be a well read man was t 3 benefit scciety. Mr. Stainer then distributed 90 prizes to the mernirrs.</p>
        <p>PICNICS TO RHYL AND EILWARDEN.</p>
        <p>The annual chnir picnic in connection with St. Paul's Church. Widnes, took place on Monday. Forty-two members of the chnir.accomnanieti by the Revs J Adams V.A., and R E Corlett, and Mr W Cooke (waist), journeyed in reserve coaches per the Londoo and North- Western Railway to Rhyl. The start was made in threatening weather, but the elnom of the sky did not damp the gaiety of the party, who departed ful!y bent on enjoying themselves to the utmost. Upon arrival at the Welsh watering place the eompony rambled abnut the town and sands until noon, when dinner was par- taken of, after which some of the members of the party visitsd the pretty church at Bodelwyd lan, and the Cathedral of St. AFaoh. By the Fame train in the morn- ing the m-mbers rf th- S'. Miry's Women's Bible Class, to the number of about 60, travelled to Hawarden. On arrival at their destination the class was met by Mrs. Teller, who escorted the members through the pictur- esque little village and over the grounds GE Hawarden Castle.</p>
        <p>Sr. MICHAEL'S CHURCH, RUNCORN; The annual choir sermons were preached on Sunday. In the m.rning the ru'pit was occupied by the Rev. C. R. KiHick (vicar of Holy Trinity), and in the evening the sermon was nreached by the Rev. W. Colston, curate of Frodsham. The afternoon was devoted to a flower service, at which the Rev H. B. Firth gave a seasonable adderss. The flowers contributed by the scholars were subsequently distributed among the sick people in the Parish. At th, morning service the choir sang the TA Deum to a composition by Monro. The Magnificat and None Dimittis at evensong were by Turner, the treble and has. solos receiving sympathetic treatment from Master Percy Ridge and Mr W Flopw,ed. The anthem. "Oh, give thanks " was also rendered by the choir. Mr W Grounds presided at the organ. The offertories amounted to over £9. It would b. a great pity to lose this opportunity of calling attention to the quality of Master Percy Ridge's voice ; it is exceptional, and St. Michael's are rich in the possession of so efficient a</p>
        <p>A THREATENING PRISONER AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Befere Mr. John Ocklestnn, el Friday. Margaret Antrobes, an inmate of the Runcorn Union Workheuse, Diftton, was charged in custody with breaking a pane of glass, value ls.. on the 28th June.—Prisoner pleaded gui!te.—Mr. Johnson explained that the woman had been remanded from the previous day, so that an oppor- tunity could be given for a medical man to examine her es to her mental condition .—Dr. McDougall said he saw the woman, but there did net appear to be anything wrong with her besides an ungovernable temner. He would like to suggest, however, that a too rigid discie- line might be in some way responsible for these violent outbreaks. Given a lemeer time in which to observe her there might be discovered a " weak spot." still. h. was of the opinion that a little kindly treatnrent would go a long way.—Thomas Crempten. an inmate of the Work- house, said he had charge of the Tramn Ward. On the 29th ult., the woman who was confined in No. 9 cell broke a window.—Prisoner said she was" put up to it e' "they" were not satisfied until she did something of that kind, so tiles she could be sent to Knutsfer I.— Superintendent Pearsen stated that the woman during the time she was confined in the cells had acted as a mad woman. screaming violently the whole time, and she had taken all the head work off her clothing.— After a lengthy consultation with the Docter, the magis- trate committed prisoner to Knutsford for ledays. with- out hard labeur.—Prisoner sullenly said she would not gn. and if she did " there would be a murder or some- thin of that kind on her cornier, out."—Refusing to leave the dock, ehe was forcibly taken below• A LARGE SHIP AT RUNCORN. On Tuesday afternoon there was moored at Runcorn las -by. the largest sailing ship that has yet entered the Ship Canal. This was the Andorinha, four masted, a steel barque,3so feet lone,and with a draught of 23 feet. She has a net registered tonnage ef 3.264 tons, and she has a cargo of 5,200 tons, or 1.700 standards of deals from: ILlpewelli CapelNew Brunswick. The dimensions of the ship will he b-etee understood when it is stated that the Andorinha is 50 fe.tronzer than the Meltonburn and a thousand tons more eerreine capacity. The ship left Hopewell Cape. on the 7th June, in charge of a pilot, but it was not until the 15th June that she got clear of the bay, owing to fog and head wind. From Cape Table to Eestham loll( was accomplished in the smart time ef 14 days—a re- markably quick pessage. Captain Daniel Nicholas of Swansea, described his ship as the longest British sailing ship alloat and he is proud of her sailing qualities. In October last year. the shin was on fire at New York and she vet bear; evidences of the conflagration in charred decks It". The bulwarks on the starboard side are twisted in most einuous form by the action of the heat. The ship was condemued and was purchased by Messrs. John A. Jacobs and Company, of London, who had new gearing nut on and the frame of the ship tested, and hid her towed to New Brunswick for the cargo siie has brought to Runcorn, and which will be transhippei into lieeters and conveyed to Manchester: The envies, un the Shin Canal was accomplished without unueual incident, the ship beine in charge of three trigs.</p>
        <p>BR sure and TRY ONE of J -BATLEY ISlkeB-PORK PIES. They are a luxnry.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>VOYAGE UP THE SHIP • CANAL. THE URBAN COUNCIL AND THE CANAL COMPANY.</head>
        <p>A VOYAGE UP THE SHIP • CANAL. THE URBAN COUNCIL AND THE CANAL COMPANY.</p>
        <p>HARMONIOUS ITN DERST iiNDI NG.</p>
        <p>At the invitation of Mr. F. Wiawall C.E., J.P., chairman of Runcorn Urban District Council, the members of that body and a number of local gentle- men interested in trade had quite a new departure in the way of Mayoral ho3pitality. A banquet of course of the "recherche type" is the usual method of signalising elevations to the chair but Mr. Wiswall s celebration consist ed oga pleasant voyage up the Ship Canal in a saloon boat and a return by way of Brindley's creation "the Bridgewater canal" and a luncheon of a most gratifying and satisfying character. All this by the way by the kind per- missionof Mr. J. K. Bythell, the Chairman of the Ship Canal Company. The company embarked on the boat at bottom locks opposite Bridgewater House at 9.30 a.m. and having passed safely through the locks the craft was taken in tow by the steaming "Lymm" and the cleavage of the some- what murky water of the Ship Canal was immedi- ately commenced, The company wan made up as follows :—Messis F Wiswall the host, J Oldfield, superintendent of the Bridgewater Canals, S Taylor 3P. F J Noran JP. A R Norman S 1", w Timmins JP, Jckleston JP, E Imison CC, W Handley, J Wilson, T Dean, J Horsefield, F Johnsen, magistrates' clerk, D Pearson, superin- tendent of police, H J Grice, J W Lightburn, James Pritchard, M J Sweetman, G Hewitt, W Collier, T Jones, D Dunning, representing the Anderton Com- pany. D Bisbrown, W R Toft, E Gatley, T H Kirk- ham, S Walton, 3 M Frith, Dr McDougall, medical officer of health, F A Lake, law clerk, W Sutton, as- sistant overseer, E Marshall, secretary, W Jones chief clerk, J Wilding, surveyor, T A Lawrenson, principal of Technical Institute, and T C Haddock, manager of Parr's Bank and treasurer to the Coun- cil. A bright sun, a pleasant breeze, and congenial companionship, made the voyage extremely pleasant, and the various points of interest were viewed, bat as Latchford was ap- proached an aroma not altogether agreeable assailed the senses, and it did not improve, the nearer the Cotton City was approached. The various bridges were objects of admiration, and Latchford Locks safely negotiated the next point of interest was the Weir at Woolston which had solved a difficulty which threatened to put the Canal Company to much litigation and great expenee. To the skilled intelligence and inventive brain of Mr Wiswall do the company owe the un- ravelling of a knotty problem. By Act of Parlia- ment it was stipulated that after the construction of the Ship Canal the waters of the Mersey should leave the canal at Rixton Junction and flow down its natural course through Warrington. During construction provision had not been made for this and not only flood water but a good deal of the natural flow of the river want through the sluices at Latchford Locks In times of flood this was a source of danger to the navigation of the lower reaches of the canal and in addition robbed Warrington of much needed water. Although Mr. Wiswall's department is the Bridge- water undertaking he was called in and his scheme was adopted in spite of great opposition. Mr. Wis- wall took out two solid weirs, one at Butchersfield and one at Woolston,and in their place constructed his patent tilting weir which has received great praise from Sir Frederick Bramwell, president of the Institute of Engineers, which served for both the discarded weirs. To describe the technical details of the weir would serve no good Purpose, but it presents many unique featuree. and it may be stated that its effectiveness has been demonstrated to the fullest extent. When closed the weir is like an ordinary weir, but when open Presents no obstruction to the water whatever, and flood waters proceed on their course without let or hindrance. Leaving here,a splendid luncheon pre- pared in first-class style by Mr. and Mrs. Goulden, of Bridgewater House, was partaken of' and Barton aqueduct being reached, this great feat of engineer- ing skill was inspected with deep interest, and the host gave some interesting facts in connection with its construction. The grain elevators at Manchester—huge wooden buildings of American construction—excited the wonder of the party. The elevator has a capecity of 40,000 tons or 1,500.000 bushels having 226 bins and the machinery is on such a scale that the following operations can be performed simultaneously (a) dis- charging from vessels in the dock at the rate of 350 tons per hour, (b) weighing in the tower at the wrieer's edge; (c) conveying to the house and dis- tributing into any of the 226 bins; (d) moving grain about within the house for changing bins or de- livery and weighing at the rate of 500 tons per hoot; (e) sacking grain, weighing, loading sacks into 40 railway waggons and 10 carts simultaneously; (f) conveying from the elevator into barges or coasters at the rate of 150 tons per hour if in bulk, or 250 sacks per hour if bagged. The danger of an outbreak of fire struck most of the visitors, who were informed that besides mach hose, and many buckets, there were distributed about the building 5,000 sprinklers, capable of flooding the building with water. While the party had been inspecting this interesting building, the eriloen boat had been taken to Throstles Nest, on the Bridgewater Canal, and here the entire company again boarded the craft, and assembled in the commodious and confortahle saloon, and a few toasts were honoured as the boat progressed towards Run- corn. Then tea was served and the rest of the even- ing was spent in viewing the ever changing lands- cape as the boat glided through the rippling water, Rancorn being reached shortly before ten. Every- body voted the trip one of a most enjoyable character.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>SPEECHES.</head>
        <p>THE SPEECHES.</p>
        <p>The Chairman gave the toast of the Queen which was dulv honoured.</p>
        <p>Mr. Taylor. vice-chairman of the Council asked the Company to join him in drinking succe,s to the Skip Canal Company. He was sure he could speak for every member of the Council and others present that day that they felt most grateful to the Company for giving them that enjoyable trip, and he coupled the name of Mr. Oldfield with the toast. (Applause.) He had known Mr Oldfield for a number of years and there was no one connected with the Ship Canal Company that had the interests of the cemoany more at heart than Mr Oldfleld and he had found him in business transactions a most fair, straightforward, genial and affable gentleman. (Hear, hear.) It was no news to them thet as far as Runcorn was concerned there was no authrritv on the banks of the Ship Canal that bad had less difficulty with the company and had given less trouble and anxiety than the Runcorn Urban Authority. (Hear, hear.) It had been the wish of the Runenrn Authority from the commencement to settle amicably any clirpute that had arisen and they had had very little difficulty in doing so. The Runcorn Authority had always gone to the company in a straightforwsr 1 manner and they had never asked for anything they had no right trt expect. They had 4mrilV asked for the protection of the town in a straivhtfeer weed manner and they had consequently had ve-y few) difficulties with the company and they all hoped this would not be the last time they might have so enjoyable a treat as they had had that day. (Anplause ) The toast was drunk with great enthusiasm. The Chairman explained that Mr.Oldfi.ld had assisted very much in arranging for the trip and he mentioned this to add more weight, if that were possible, to what Mr. Taylor had said. They who were connected with the Council looked upon Mr. Oldfield as one of nature's gentlemen. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Mr. Oldfield said he felt overpowered b 7 the eloquent words that had been used in regard to him. It would :be very gratifying to their chairman (Mr. Bythell) to know that the gentlemen of Runcorn had appreciated this little trip. It was not a big' thing in itself, and why it had not been done before be did not know, because he was sure it was a very great help to the ship Canal Company to have the assistance at any time of the people of Runcorn, and the Ceuncillors of Runcorn. Perhaps up to the present time the Canal had not been of much benefit to Runcorn, but really Runcorn was the principal place on the Canal between Ra-tham and Man- chester—Warrington was outside the Canal— but the day would come and he hoped very soon, when it would have benefit from the Canal. it took time to bring round these advantages, but they must come and he booed they would came in the near future. He was that lay saying to one of the Councillors cf Runcorn that he did net see why in the course of a few years there should not be a development of works even on Frodshatn Marsh, then they would hear Runcorn Council going in for a bridge connecting Frodsham Marshes with the town of Runcorn. Prohatly Runeorn would be made into a city One of these days (Hear, hear). But they must have a Mayor and Corporation firs% (Laughter.) In regard to the flatt, ring remarks, with re- gard to himself, he did not feel that he deserved them, but if at any future time any assistance of his could afford any facility to the Council of Runcorn he ehould only be too pleased to do what little he could towards it. (Applause.) Re was aura Mr Bythel if he could only in- terest the people of Runcorn by inviting them to look at the Ship Canal would be amply rewarded. By such a visit as the one they pail that day ideas might be developed which might be to the advancement of both the Ship Canal Company and Runeern,and besides good- will would be fostered by ister-contseunieation of this</p>
        <p>kind. (Hear, hear.) The communion of ideas led to the development ot ideas,when done in a friendly spirit and not in a carping and objectionable spirit. He believed Mr. Taylor was quite right in his stattmentthat Runcorn had at all times helped the Ship Canal to their utmost. There had been differences but they had only been natural differences when a township had had to look after its own interests and if Councillors did not look alter the interests of the town they represented they would not be worth the position entrusted to them. (Hear, hear.) In conclusion Mr Oldfield thanked them for their appreciation of what little he had done. (Applause.) _</p>
        <p>• _ Dr. McDougall said he was sore the days proceedings could not be allowed to t2rminate without expressing their deep appreciation of Mr. Wiswall's supervision and interest in their behalf. (Hear, hear.) As Chairman of tbe Council a position he worthily filled and as an engineer Mr. Wis wall had invited them to this delight- ful trip which they had all greatly appreciated. They had had a most pleasant trip along the Ship Canal and were now passing down the older canal which was so very much associated with the early history of Runeora. Runcorn was between both canals and they hoped and believed that in the time to come not only would the Ship Canal continue to grow and flourish but that Run- corn would reap benefit for the sympathy and interest which it had always Invested in the Ship Canal. (ape plause.)Comine along the Ship Canal he was much inter- este] in the Woolston Weir. Mr. Wiswall was not aware of the fact that he knew something of the history of the difficulties between the Canal Company and the Warrington Corporation, mad he did not mind allowing Mr. Wiswall to know a conversation that took place between Alderman Southern and himself in his own house after Mr. Wiswall came to the rescue of the Ship Canal and provided the weirs at Woolston. Alder- man Southern said the Company were indebted:entirely to Mr. Wiswall for the invention and introduction of these weirs. (Hear. hear.) He was pleased to think they had resident in Runcorn a gentleman representing the in- terests of the Ship Canal who could give such material help in surmounting a great difficulty. In conclusion Dr. McDougall assured Mr. Wiswall that thcy had all greatly enjoyed and deeply appreciated his hospitality that day and he askaJ the cempany to drink to his long life and prosperity. (Applause.) Mr. Oldfield endorsed Dr. McDougall's reference to Woolston weir. Mr. Wiswall never received the credit he ought to have received publicly, for his paet fa that difficult task. Mr. Wiswall had great opposition to his scheme to face, but his views were sound and workable, and he stood a very great test, and came out of it in splendid style, and he had never had the credit be de- served. (Hear, hear.) When Mr. Wiswall invented that self-acting weir, it stood the test at various places, and perfectly prevented the floods at Broughtoa, Crumpsall and other places to which they had been accustomed. The toaat was drunk with mnusicti honours.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wiswall said he was much obliged for this testi- mony of their satisfaction of what he in his humble way had done that day. It ought to have been a success, considering he had been three or four years thinking about it. (Laughter.) He had had it Le. his mind several years, and when he became chairman of the Council he thought it was his duty to bring it about in some way—(hear, hear)—and the moment he mentioned it to Mr. Oldfield it was as good as done. He must acknowledge the very kind and flattering remarks Dr. McDougall and Mr. Oldfield had made with regard to himself. It was true as stated that he had tee privilege of getting the Ship Canal Co. out of a serious difficulty. He not only formulated the scheme, tut he did the work. (Hear, hear.) It was a special kind of work to which he bad been accustomed. It was a simple matter really because it was part of a scheme he had for improving the old Mersey and Ir well navigation, on which he had started before the Ship Canal was commenced, and had not the Ship Canal been made, that navigation would have been improved. Howeeer, the Ship Cunt was made, and it would remain for evf r a benefit to Lanca- shire and Cheshire, and even the whole country. (Hear, hear). Mr. Oldfieici said it was a matter of hiltorical know- ledge that Mr. Wiswall's scheme for widening the river was under way. About £24,000 had teen spent as an earnest in carrying out the scheme which would have meant a total outlay of about £350,000 and the Bridge- water Navigation shareholders were willing to spend the money. But Mr. Wiswall was better out of the field by a big cheque for £1,710,003. If that cheque had not been paid Mr. Wis wall would have made the river navigation capable of carrying vessels of 500 tons to Manchester. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>ANNIVERSARY SERVICES.-0a Sunday the annual ser- vices on behalf of the United Methodist Free Church Sunday School were conductcd by the Rev. R. Percival, the pastor, but owing to the inclemency of the weather the congregations were not so large as usuol. Special hymns were rendered by the chair, and the offertories for the day realised £l3 ll.s. lid.</p>
        <p>SUNHAT Scaoot Excuastois..—On Wednesday the schools in this parish were all closed on account of Sunday school excursions. The elder scholars and teachers of the Church-street Church Sunday school(Miss Crostield's), accompanied by the Rev R Coiston and Mr John Barrow (superintendent), had their annual excursion, Rhyl being the destination. A. special train was procund,and fully 200 joined it at 9 am. Dinner and tea were partaken of at the Rhyl Town Hail, the return journey was com- menced st.on after 8, Frodsham being reached before 10 o'clock. A. heavy thunderstorm broke out during the early part of the evening, which necessitated sheltering en the part of the juveniles, but for all that a very pleasant day was spent, through the usual liberality and kindness of Miss Crosfield. The scholars and teachers of the Trinity Wesleyan Chapel Sunday School drove in the afternoon in 'busses and other vehicles, generously lent by townspeople, to Delamere Forest, where tea was served at Mr • Hardman's, after which games were indulged in in a large room. DEATH OF MR. WILLIAM HATES.—We regret to an- nounce the death of Mr. William Hayes (son of the late Mr. William Hayes, of Frodsham, and also of Ashton Hayes, Bear Chester), which t aok place on Sunday last, at Watford Manor, Shrewsbury, at the age of 45. Mr. Hayes was connected in no small way with the colliery business, and also in the firm of Messrs. Brundrit and Co.. et Penmaenmawr.—The funeral took place on Thursday afternoon at Overton Church. Fr=dshem.</p>
