      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT. FRlDLY.—Before Mr. S. Wright (in the chair), and Coun- cillor Smethurst. MINOR CASES.—James Frederick Maddock, of Pendleton. was charged with being drunk in charge of two horses and a lurry in Manchester-road the previous night.—Defendant said it was the fits that were plaguing him:—Fined 5s includ- ing costs or seven daya.—George Flannigan was fined 5s and costs or seven days, for being drunk and disorderly in Dallam-lane the previous night.--Y.C. Smith proved the case -Catherine Richardson was similarly fined for a like off ence. A BLREFooTED BEGGlR.—Michael Glynn was charged with begging in Manchester-road the previsma night.—The Con- stable, who proved the case, said he arrested prisoner at the order of the Chief Constable.—Superintendent Monaghan said the man called at the house of the Chief Constable amongst others. He was drunk and barefooted. The prisoner got his living by begging but was well able to werk.—The Bench committed him t 3 prison for 14 days with hard labour the Chairman remarking that he would treat all such cases in a like manner if he had his own way. A CHILD'S WANDERINGs.—WiIIiam Winstanley was charged with deserting his child on the 31st May and hewing it chergeable to the Common funds of the Warrington Union.—Mr. Shearer said the child was one year and eight months old and was admitted to the Workhouse on the 31st May as it had been left at its grandmother's door by the defendant. The child was taken to him somewhere in the neighbourhood of Leigh but he broueht it back to Earlestown and would hsve nothing to do with it. It was then taken to the Workhouse. The mother had previously deserted the child . —Defendant said he would keep the child if they eyould give him an opportunity.—Mr. Shearer consented to an adjournment being made for four weeks to give the defendant a chance. SATURDA.Y.—Before Mes=rs. Birchen, R. T. Falrcleugh and J. W. Hayward. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Tut following persons were each fined for being drunk and disorderly on Friday : —John King, Its including costs ; John Kelly, 53 and costs; Thomas Peel and Mary Ann Tapia. 5s and costs each: James Jordan, 5s and costs; Kate Ann Jones, 13s and casts ; Amelia Long- shaw, 5s and costs. A DRUNKEN PRELK.—William Clare was charged with being drunk in Ship-yard, and breaking 20 panes of glass, value As 6d, the property of Mrs McVerdic.—Prosecurrix said shei kept a registered lolging-house in Ship-yard. Prisoner came to her house last night about six o'cle ck, He was in a drunken con, ition, but had loigee there before. Prisoner took up a piece of iron and smashed 23 panes of glass.—P.S. Evans deposed to apprenhending pirsoner. He said that he wanted to breasc some more, but wes stopped.— The Chlef Constable said that prisoner was a stranger.—A penalty of lOs and costs was imposed. SCENE IN PERCIVAL'S-YARD.—John Holker was charged with haying been drunk and disorderly in Percival's-yard at 12.15 on Friday night He was also charged with assaulting Mrs. Gleason, and P.C. Dennett.—Mrs. Gleason said that prisoner, who lodged at her house, went there very drunk and made a disturbance. She seat a boy for the police.— P.O Bennett said he went te Pereneal's-yard and saw enieeisatec ...I.enhing to Sigh:, and he was using very bad language. Prisoner kicked witness's lamp about and threatened to kick his "ribs" in. He was like a mad dog.— Fined 511 and costs. MONDAY,—Before the Major (in the chair). Alderman Black- hurst. Councillors; Shaw Green and Smethurst, Mr. S. Wright, and Mr. T. Birchall. TEMPORARY AtrnioßrrY.--William Carlton Beecham ap- plied for temporary authority to sell at 63, Priestly-street in lieu of James Beechem.—The application was granted. Demur inn DISoRDERLY.—fhe following were fined for being drunk and disorderly : - Sarah Ann Finn, 3, Dakin's- yard, 5s including costs ; John and Masgaret Barker, 4, Morris-court, 5s including costs each; jemes Corcoran, ss. including costs. NIGHT SCENIC IN RTLINDS•STREET —*mum Dudley, was charged with having been drunk anel disorderly in Rylands- street, at 10.25 ou Saturday night, and also with assaulting PC Taylor.—P tI Taylor said that he was leaving duty at the Royal Court Theatre, when he saw the prisoner on his back In the street in the midst of snout 100 people. Witness told him to go home. but defendant kicked him. He blew his whistle, and P C Plumb came to his assistance and prisoner was arrested. He was very violent on the way to the Police Station.—P C Plumb also gavel videnea in support of the charge. The Chief Constable said th • man was a stranger in the town; he came from Widnes —He was sentenced to one month's imprisonment with hard labour. PITCH AND Toss.—Lawrence Hannon (12), 23, Yereeste and John Smith (11), 84, Orford-lane. were charged with tossing tor coins in Hardy-street. on Sunday, June 25th._ P.O. Clough stated that he was in plain clothes in company with P.C. Wright. They watched the prisoners from an entry and saw them play a number of games. —Mr. Birchall said it was getting a regular tking now for beys to play at pitch and toss on Sunday. He bad heard many complaints. and he had personally offered a reward for the conviction of persons who played pitch and toss. Fined 53 ea6l.—Samuel and John Millington, 36, Cookhedge-lane, and John Henry Dagnall, printer, 6. Garden-row, were also charged with tos- sing for coins on Sunday, June 251h.—Pris00ers said they were playing with some marbles. Fined 5s each including costs. 'Bye CONDUCTORyINED.—Isam Wadsworth, 15, Cartwright- street. was fined 5s for allowing three passengers to ride on the footstep of the 'bus on the 25-11 June —t'.o. Clark proved the case. BREACH OF THE PEACE.—William Henry Darby, 9 Brown- street, Arthur Bromley. 4, Brown-street, Frank Miller, 21 Dale-street, Thomar Kelly, 17. Dale-street William Whaling 75, Howard-street, George Smith, 144, Liverpool-road and Herbert Jenkins, 8. Wright-street were charged with com- mitting a breaeb ef the peace by having been disorderly in Ground-street and Howard-street on the 24th June. P.C.Wood stated that he saw the prisoners over yard walls. He was in an entry, and also saw them throwing bricks at some doors. He caught Darby and obtained the other boys' names and addresses. They were altogether and he ssnaw them well.— They were bound over to keep the peace for six months and to pay the costs. THRIWING STONES AT GAS LAMPi.—Janits Brooks (10), 258, Winwick-road ; Husisou (10). 5 Warburton, street ; Thomas Bracegirdle (10), 6. Warburton-street ; James Tranter (10). 2 Warburton-street; were charged wills breaklne the glass isi a gas lamp in Diss'etm-lann the property of the , Corporations. - George Somerfet (13) said that on June 22nd he saw the boys throwing stones at a gas lamp in Dallam-lane at about 9.30 p.m. He saw them break the g ass. He told them shout it, ani they used very bad language.—P.C. Clough said he made inquiries about the lamp aud succeeded in obtaining the prisoners' names and addresses. They admitted the offence. A great amount of damage had been dons lately ' about Dallarn-lane to lamps.—Mr. Taylor (secretary, to the Gas Works) stet -d that the damage would be about 6s. Mr Simpson (Dheshire Lines) said that they had a number of carriage windows broken ny boys throwing at the trains and on one or two occasions I guards had bean Injured. —Mr Birchen thanked the boy Somerset for giving the evidence in such a straightforward manner. It was a serious offence and defendants would be fined 5s each.</p>
        <p>TUESDIY.—Beforo Mr. Garnett (in the chair), anl Mr. Birchall.</p>
        <p>... " A GREAT NUISANCE IN TUE Tow,r."—Mary Brown, Dial- street, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Clieshire's-vard Winwiek•street, at 11-15 on Monday night. —P.C. Gibbs proved the ease, and said he bad to ask a soldier to help him t bring her to the station.—The Chief Oonstible sici she had been before the Salford an 4 other magistrates 22 times, and several times before th. Warrington Bench. She was a great nuisance to the town. —She was sent to prison for one month with hard labour. AN EARL'o ST MN "iIAN FIN ED.—William Ward of 19,Bradley- roan, Harlestown, was charged with being drunk and dis- orderly in Bridge-street at 5.45 on Monday nlgnt.—He pleaded Tay!or said he saw the &amp;fern-tint in Bridge• street, knocking against people. He followed him in o ths Rose and Crown Inn, where be was refused. arink and on</p>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
      </div>
