        <p>iof43ifix3fili</p>
        <p>BURTONWOOD MEN FixEn.—Jesse Dickson and William Malkin, young men, were summoned for having on the 25:h June, at Burtunwood, damaged a quantity of oats growing in a field in the occupation of Jol.n Herriman, doing damage to thr extent of a shilling.—The case was proved and tue defendants were fined 2s 6,1 each and ordered to pay th 3 damage. BREACH OF FACTORY REGULLTIoNS. —Joseph Daintith was summoned for having on the 18th Jauivry faile I to report an accident which bad ocsurred in his workshop to a lad in kis employ—Mr. Rogers, factory inspector, said he appeared for the tame Office. The defendant was charged with a breach of the Factory Regulttions which prescribed that all accidents should be notified to the inspector of the district— all accidents which kept employees away from work for five hours afterwards. On the 25th May an inspector of the department called at the works of the defendant, who is a wheelwright carrying on business at Croft, and noticed that one of the boys who was at work had lost a couple of fingers. Inquiries were made and it was found that the accident occurred about the middle of January, when the lad was working at a c,rts.in machine. It was very importint that notices of accidents should be sent to the inspector, because then when they heard of them they were usually able to make arrangements to prevent a recurrence —The defendant stated that the inspector would not have known of the accident had he not told him of it.—The Clerk (Mr.Henty Gireenall) : That was after the event.—A fine of 5s and costs was imposed.</p>
        <p>AS9AULT IN A PENKETH PUBLIC HOUSE.—James Sloan wa summoned for having on the 17tn June assaulted Robert Littlewool.—Both are respectable men and well dressed.— the complainant stated that on the 17th June he want into fte Crown and Cushion at Penketh, when he met the de- fendant, who commenced to quarrel with him as well as with another mut. Both were strangers to him Witness told the defendant they were in a civilised country and that his conduct was font of reason." The defend-eat then rushed at him, butted him on his licad and temple and cut his breast He afterwards saw the defendant in the street when the as sault was repeated —ln answer to the defendant complainan stated that -he never laid a finger" on Sloan.—Defendant He got hold of rae by the back of my neck and shoved me against a corner. Both he and his wi.e also insulted me.— The Chairman said the Bench bad decided to clnfine them- selves to the assault in the house, and the defendant wonid be fined ss.</p>
        <p>MINOR CA.SES.—joseph Robinson was fined Ts 6d for allow- ing his dog to be at lirge unmuzzled at Sankey.—Jessie Monks claimed the sum of 21 12s from David l'aylor,Newton Common, for services reodere,4 to him as laundry maid, and an order was made for payment of that arnount,the defendant not appearing.—Emily Poyey claimed 2,1 53 from he defend- ant and it was allowed.—John Stevenson, charged with using threats to Eleanor Ormston, at Winwick, on the let July, was bound over to keep the peace for six months. ASSAULT ON A PoiacEmarc BY A NEWTON MlN.—Ralph fluid, a middle aged min, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly at Newton, on the 4th July, and with assmiting P.C. Samuel Glascoyne whilst acting in the execu- tion of his duty at the Earlestown Police Station.—P.C. Ben- son deposed to finding the prisoner drunk and fighting at Newton. He asked him to go hems, but Lie refused to do so and he was compelled to take him to the Harlestown Police Station and look him up.—P.O. Go.scoyne said that he visited the prisoner in his cell last night and asked him how he was getting on and if he was all right, when he c3mmenced to create a (disturbance. He rushed at witness, struck him a violent blow on the eye and also tnrew him to the ground.— A fine of 103 and costs was inflicted.</p>
        <p>CHARGE AGAINST A DOMESTIC SERVINT.—A. girl named Ma-y Singleton was chariten with stealing a shirt, a bodice, a quantity of tea and half-a-crown, valued at Us. sometime between ten p.m. on the 27th June and six a.m, on the 28th June the property of Ralph Green.the licensee of the General Elliot Hotel, Crvdt.—Mrs Green stated that she was the wife of Ralph Green, the licensee of the hotel, and the prisoner had been in her employ eleven days as a domestic servant. The skirt and bodice the prisoner was wearing belonged ta wane s. The prisoner left her house on the 27th June.— Detective McLeunon,stationed at Leigh,said he apprehended the prisoner on bunday night, and when he charged hr with the robbery she replied " I did not take anything only what I have here." She was then wearing the bodice and skirt she was now wearing, and which had been identified by Mrs Green as her propettv. The prisoner had been in custody since Saturday night, She was brought bafore the Leigh magistrates on Monday and committed to the Warrington County Petty Sessions.—The prisoner asked what she had to say in her defence, replied that she und. rstood the garments had been given to her by her mistress as she was instructed to repair them. She went away beoause she was under the impression she had been discharged.—The Chair nen m We think there is no evidence to ceny,ct, and the prisoner will be discharged.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PET rY SE3SIONI.</p>
        <p>MoNDAY.—Bet ore Messrs,. A. Thomas (prasiding) and</p>
        <p>W. Timmins.</p>
        <p>THE DIFFICULTIES or A "CHIP" 6/10P.—Thomas Fearnett, High-street, Runcsrn was summoned for keeping open his refreshment house during prohitite i hours on the 24th June. —Defendlnt pleaded guilty.—Police4:lonstable James Smith (Thernton) said he was on duty in High-street on Saturday night. June 24th. Defendant was the occupier of a "chip potato restaurant." Seeing the place opened at 11.30 he in c,mpany with Constable Thomas dwards vielted the premises. They found a number of men there being served. tin calling the defendant's notice to the fact that the place should have been closed at 11 o'clock, he replied "Take no notice and I will send them out." Defendant did not do so; and witness saw Wiser men go in after wards who were also served. Witness then said he would report the matter, to which defendant replied: "He did not care a b—; he was going to keep open until he sold his perishable stuff." adding "Me supposed witness was one of those fast b— like the other that was sent there?" Witness did not know to whom he referred.—Constable Uetwards,Runeorn, corroborated.—Sir J. Donneilan was called to prove that defendant was licensed to keep a refreshment house.—Defendant said he would admit that.—For his defence he submitted 114 hal, never been proceeded against in respect to anything but the one offence that of "keeping open." He found that during the summer months people did not retire so early ; and his business being absolutely a " supper business" it was often enough that he never commenced to sell his stuff until nine o'clock at night. He had spent over £lOO on his inside business If he had no license he could keep opals until 12 o'clock, and he had been considering whether to give up his inside busi- ness and his license. He had been wavering, but at length leas comoellea to the conclusion the`, the remedy was worse than the disease. If he was not granted sufficient time to dispose of his perishable stuff, it not only meant his Rot selling it. but It would have to be thrown away.—The Chair- man : You know that your place should be closed w 11 o'clock.—Defendant: Yes, th:t is a technical breach accord- ing to the letter of the law.—The Chairman : Well that is What we have to go by. You will be fined lOs and 175 6,1 Coats, or 14 days in default. The Money was paid. A DANGEROUS PRAOTICE.—JO3OOI AellOrOft, 13 years of age, of 3, King-street. was summoned for throwing stones in Cooper-street to the danger of passengers on the 23rd of June. —He pleaded guilty to throwing stones, but said there were no passengers.—Police Constable John Wilding said he saw the boy throwing stones from Cooper-street to Mill-street over the houses.—Fined 55. PROFANE LANGUAGE.—IsaUeIIit XIAZOAII, for using profane language in Back High-street on the 2ith of Juße,was tined Sc and 8e 61- costs.--P.O. H Fry prove t the cue. SCHOSL CASES.—Samuel Dutton was fined Ss for neglecting the education of his daughter fistlier.—His wife, who ap- pearee asked for time to pay. Seven days Were allowed. DRUNK AND A.SLEE ,IN HIGH-EVREET —llaury Booth, having inbibed too mush lisle, on Saturday (July Ist) thougnt he would sleep in High-street, on the parapet opposite Dr. McDougall's. Constabte Edwards in the course or his patrol, came across him and roused nim ui. Ascertain- ing that he lived" up areenwey-road "the Constable started Booth off in that direction. Beoth, however, tried to do an- other sleep in Greenway-road and again chose a berth opposite a doctor's (Dr, McKenzie). The police offiser then thought Booth had better be provided with eleepiag accarnmodetion, and quietly walked hit&amp; off to the " lock-up."—Tne sequel was, Booth got fined Ss for being drunk.</p>
