      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>DEATH FROM EXCESIFE DRINKING</head>
        <p>DEATH FROM EXCESIFE DRINKING</p>
        <p>AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>THE CORONER'S ADVICE.</p>
        <p>The death of Mary Quinn, a married woman, 25 years of age, took place on Friday night lasts. It appears that Quinn, who was the wife of 'frank Quinn, a canal boat labourer living in Arnold's-yard, off Hardock-street, was put into an out building in the yard at six o'clock on Friday night t) "sleep off the effects of drink." When the husband reached home at nine he asked for his wife. He was told where she was. He at once went to the place named, but great was his surprise to find that she was dead.</p>
        <p>The inquest on the body was held in the parade room at the police station on Monday afternoon, before the deputy-coroner, Mr. F. A. Jones. The husband of the deceased was the first witness called. He stated that he had been married only three weeks. He did not call his wife a drunken woman but be had seen her under the infleence of drink three or four times. His wife let home on Tnursday on a visit to hr sister, who was about to go on a visit to Ireland. He was in Bridge-street on Friday afternoon when he met the deceased, who was the worse for drink. He said "You look well to go to your sisters," and the reply was" lam net going," AA his request she went home, but witness did not do so. However, he returned about nine o'c'eck and asked a Mrs. Groves, a neighbour, where his wife was. He was told she was in the outbuilding. He went to the place but could not open the door as her feet blocked it. With great difficulty he at last succeeded in getting ieto the building, and found his wife lying on the floor. She was carried into the house and he called her name several times, but there was no response. She had had fits. Be at once went for Dr. Curtis, who on his arrival stated that she was dead. He had known the deceased five years before they were married, and during that time she had been given to drink. The Coroner: Why did you marry a woman who was given to drink? Witness (with tears in his eyes) : I thought a great deal about her, and I did not care to see her le;t. Both her father and mother were dead. The Coroner: It was a wasted kindness to ma.rry:a woman under such circumstances. In answer to the Chief Constable, witness said his wife frequenqy went to see her sister,and on these occa- sions he x ould not see her for the whole of the day. She generally drank whisky and stout. The Coroner : Next time I advise you to marry a sober woman. _</p>
        <p>Agnes Groves, a neighbour, said the deceased came borne about 6 p.m., and she was seen to fall in the yard. She would not go into the house. They could not com- pel her to do so, and as she was a heavy woman they could not carry her. She was takan to the outtuilding where she remained about three hours. She was not aware until the husband arrived that the woman re- mained the whole of that time in the place.—ln answer to the Chief ConstAb'e, witness stated that the deceased fell on the back of her head in the yard Pretty heavily. She was "helplPse with beer?' The Coroner : Did you think it was anything more than a drunken fit? Wituess : No, sir. Dr. Curtis, Bewsey-street, said the huthani of the de• ceased came to him on Friday evening at 9-30. He at once went to the house in Havdock-street, but he found that the woman was dead. She had been dead at least an kour. Drink was the cause of death so far as he could see. She had had nothing to eat and hal been drinking heavily al day. There was no sign of a fit. If she had had one it would not have been apparent when he visited her.</p>
        <p>By the Chief Constable : /The fall in the yard might have accerated the death,; but it w:)uld have hem difficult to cause it in uch a short tinv.". It was speci- fically caused by drink. The jury returned a verdict accordingly.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S TRIP DAY. --- MESSRS. RICHMOND AND COMPANY AT BLACKPOOL.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO MR. AND MRS. RICHMOND.</p>
        <p>Once again the emplove3 of Messrs. Richmond and Company Limited together with their wives and friends paid a visit to Blackpool on the oc- casion of their annual excursion. These annual outings have now become a well-atablished insti- tution in connection with Messrs. Richmond and Company's works, but additional interest was lent to this year's function by the presentation of a silver cradle to Mr. and Mrs. Richmond in honour of the birth of a son. About 700 availed themselves of the excursion and entrained in a special which left the Central Station at 6.20. On reaching Ain- tree engines were changed and the excursion then. proceeded on the Lancashire and Yorkshire railway to Blackpool. Dark and lowering clouds were met with on the way, and the outlook for a pleasant day was anything but promising On reaching Rlackpool, however, a wonderful change for the better took place, and it is a matter for congratulation that not a drop of rain was ex- perienced during the day's stay at Blackpool. With commendable forethought Mr. and Mrs. Richmond had provided breakfast for the visitors at the restaurants of Messrs. Clarke and Heap and on the arrival of the train there was a rush for these establishments.</p>
        <p>PREsENrATION TO MR. AND MRS. RICUMOND.</p>
        <p>After breakfast an adjournment was made to the Winter Gardens where the presentation was made on behalf of the employes by Mr. H. E. Ransome. the energetic manager of the Warrington works. Mrs. Richmond was unfortunately unable to be present, but her tbanks were gracefully tendered to the gathering by her winsome daughter, MiES Dorothy Richmond. There were present on the platform—Mr. Richmond and Miss Richmond. Mr. and Mrs. H. E. Ransome, Mr. H. P. Hughes (secretary), Mr. L. A. Voisey (accountant), and Mr. Charles Green kSheffield). The last-named gentleman was the designer of the cradle which was of an elaborate description. On the front there were handpainted miniature por- traits of Mr. and Mrs. Richmond ; on the back, a view of Warrington Works, and at each end a repret--entation of the firm's' Cooker "and " Royal Sovereign." The inscription on the cradle was as follows :—" Annual Outing. Blackpool, July Ist, 1899. Presented by the employ 83 of the Warrington Works of Messrs. Richmond and Co., Limited, to Mr. and Mrs. E. W. T. Richmond, to commemorate the birth (23ri of May, 1899) of their second son, Norman Richmond."</p>
        <p>In making the presentation Mr. RANSOME said they were met to present to Mr. and Mrs. Richmond a little token of respect and esteem from the Warrington men. It was a great regret to them that Mrs. Richmond was not able to be present, but they had an able little sub- stitute in Miss Dorothy Richmond. who, he was glad to tell them, had come as her mother's representative. In presenting the cradle to Mr. Richmond, he was pleased to be able to say that it bad been subscribed for most willingly and spontaneously. The sub,criptions had come in better than the most sanguine could have et- petted. He felt that Mr. and Mrs. Richmond would estoem the gift the more for knowing that it had been given so willingly by the Warrington men, and tie was also glad to be able to say that the money was sub- scribed without any appeal to outsiders. (Applause.) It spoke well for the esteem in which they were held by the men. When the idea was first mooted many of the men had a clear recollection of kindnesses received from Mrs. Richmond, who would always be remem- bered with pleasure and have the hearty good wishes of the Warriegton men. (Applause.); He felt they had not always the opportunity of present- ing a cradle of this sort, and he would ask Mr. Rich- mondlnot to look:upon it as an annual event.(Laughter). He had very great pleasure in presenting the cradle with the hearty good wishes of the Warrington men for Mr. and Mrs. Richmond. (Applause.) Miss DOROTHY RICHMOND then said : Mother told me to thank you all very much and say howeserry she is she could net be present to receive it herself. (Applause.) Mr. RICHMOND who had a hearty reception, said he did net know it they bad all heard what his daughter had said, hut he could tell them that those little words 1 had ben rehearsed all the way from London and most of the previous night. Everyone in the party knew them by heart, and they were also known by the sera- aats at the Hydro and some of the cabmen who had driven them about. (Laughter.) He was not going tol detain them with a speech but it would he very un- gracious of him if he were not to express to them in the most hearty terms the gratitude on behalf of his wife and himself for such a magnificent expression of their good feeling towards them. The exigencies of business had necessitated his leaving Warrington and going to London, but he had a very warm place in his heart for the place they started in. (Applause.) As they knew, the largest portion of the praluction of the woz ks of Richmond and Co. came from Wartington. Their large works there, as they were quite well aware, had been taxed to their utmost to execute the extremely laroe number of orders which had been lately received. He was very glad to think that they had not only been ab:e to exceed the turnover of their previoas re- cords, though they had been really remark- able, but durina the, month of June they had very nearly doubled the sales of June, 1898. (Loud applause.) Adding the totals ot goods sold at Stratford and Warringtou in 1898 and comparing them with the total of goods sold at both p:aces in 1899 they had done nearly as much again. They had sold nearly two stoves this year where last year they sold one, and everyone would agree that that was a very remarkable result. (Applause). Be could assure them that Mrs. Richmond would be gratified to know that she had been remem- bered in such a pleasant manner by her old friends. It was a source of extreme reeret to her thae her health had not permitted her to visit them that day, but hr hoped nething would prevent her coming next year, all being well. (Applause.) When he (Mr. Richmond) first beard of this presentation he thought it was far too good of them, aud now the gift had been presented he must thank everyone encerred most heartily. Be could ()ale say, in addition, that they would look upou that token of their good feeling and hearty ,intertet in their personal welfare with very warm and very deep! feelings of gratitude, knowing that their men were able' to recognize that they (Mr. and Mrs. Richmond) really 1 had their interests at heart. (Applause.) It was ‘-ery</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>RIPE FR</head>
        <p>RIPE FR</p>
        <p>grati'sing if they really took an interest in their men to find that they recogn,z-d it. He thought the omen wise a very happy one, and such a condition of affairs spoke well for the harmonious working of a firm wherever it existed. (Applause.) He hoped the boy in whose honour this presentation had ben made would grow up to take an interest in the concern which his father bad had such a pride in developing. (Hear, hear.) He was much too young to know anything about it at present, but when he became old-r and wds able to read on that tablet the record of their interest in his arrival and future ho was sure he would have a very warm and tender regard fez those who thought of him in his veil early days. (Applause.) In conclusion. Mr. Richmond acknowledged their indebtedness to the designer for the very beautiful specimen of his art he had supplied te them. He would promise them that as an ornament it would be highly prized, and the scent of the flowers i 5 would contain would convey to himself and his fora," thoughts of those whose Rood wishes had been Out/ manifested to them. (Applause.) The officials, employes, and friends, then adjourned to another part of the gardens, where a photograph was taken by Mr. J. Birtles. of Sankey-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>DINNER AT THE PALATINE HOTEL.</head>
        <p>DINNER AT THE PALATINE HOTEL.</p>
        <p>At noon an excellent dinner was served at the Pala. tine, Hotel, before the office staff and heads of the various denarttnents. The 11,tel is now under the direction of a new manager, who has already done much to enhanee its reputation. The American lounge is a distinct feature of the hotel, and in this department Mr. C. Paul, who is specialist iu the mixing of drinks. controls an extensive business.</p>
        <p>After the repast. the usual toast was honoured. Mr. HOWARD proposed "Success to the firm of Messs Richmond and Co."</p>
        <p>Mr. J. BIIZTLES supported, and the toast vras drunk with enthusimm.</p>
        <p>Mr. RICHM,ND who was received with cheers, and the singing of" He's a jelly eood fellow," said he was very much obliged to ihm for tho cordial manner in which they had honoure the toast. He was always verY pleased to accompany them oe their annual excursion' It waS one of the few oppertunitiee he got of seeinr4 them all face to face. Tie business of toe firm took LIP a great deal of his time, and when he did get to Wat- ringtln he had very little riffle t) spare. His bnsinesS lareely consisted in going up and down the country, and he had very little time to cultivate the acquaintance of those he saw around him. He did however, feel it a privilege to know his employes personally, and he could with confidence leave the work to them. The beSt , proof that that was a wise course was the fact that the business had grown at so remarkable a pace. There a had. , been a wonderful development of the business in all directions, not only in England, but in the Colonies and other places where ears was used. He trusted theY would have a substantial share of their profits to hand over to those who were doing what they could to make the business a succesS• He believed every single man in the emp'oy of the firin could make the business more successful than it would be without his individual efforts. Ho trusted they would have A successful day's outing and go Pack tn their work all the better for it. He had held up the Warrington men as an example to the men at the Strat- ford works for the splendid manner in which tbe9 tamed up after holidays. They knew how important every hour w,is to them at the present stags. They were, practically pulled out of the place for stuff and he woula ask them L..) be back aeain by Monday morning. 06 could not close his remarks without referring to the pleasure it gave him to have Mr. Raniome by his sit.le once again, looking benefitted by his recent holiday In EZyPt. He trus'ed that Mr. Ransome's health would be as strong and well as ever it was. (Hear, hear.) lie would ask them to give Mr. Ransoine all the assistance lhoy could. (Apphuse). The gathering then dispersed, and altogether a most enj9yable day was spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>LOCAL PAILIVNY ACCIDENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PAILIVNY ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>CARRIAGES OFF 'FRE LINE AT WALTON JUNCTION. An accident occurred on the London and North- Western Railway, at Walton Junction, at a late leDur 00 Saturday night, but, unfortunately it was unattendeL' with lois of life, and very few peop!e in Warrington app.ar to heve heard of it. It appears that a train con' veying Messrs. Loyd and Lloyd's workpeople from Waelepoel to Birmingham, on Saturday, left that watering place at about 9.14 n.m., and proceeded says correspondent in the " Birroingha,n Daily Post, "to about Lia miles south of Warrington where, it was pulleo up by the signals. It rernairn° there some considerable time, and at !nsV b-gan to move slowly backwarels in the diree" don of Warrine,ton. All weet well for a fel/ moments, when without a moment's notice,the carrial in which we were seated bsgan to shake violently, 30 the whole of its occupints were thrown off their seatg• When et last the train came to a stand till, and we were Enabled to alicht, we discovered that six of carrial!es, including two saloons, were off the rails, two of them leaning dangerously over the bank, 1700/4 held up apparently by the point rods which rnn alo°4 the side of the line. Several of the people were bad shaken and bruised, some having their eyes discolourPuil We ara at a loss to understand how the statement sbC appear that we did not meet with any acc_.,ideet. We add that we reached New-street Station at 7.37 on Sunda; morning. Mr. Cooke, the stationmaster at B ink sacs the above statement is substantially correct. Soo el the paiseneers got out of the train and walked Bank Quay Station, where they remained until arravg..o ments had been completed to enable tbem to conte. their journey to Birmingham, which was not long upd layed. Several cmnplained of injuries, but when 35/tir to show them failed to do so. One roan, evide° desiring compensation, piloted to a discoloura,tion.nvot his right eye, but a careful examinatien showed,tthe the injury had not been recently inflictel. Some 0 is passengers appeared to have been badly slialcen.,;jsr. not true as stated in the morning papers, that t— riage roiled down a steep embankment.</p>
