      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <p>THE EXAMINER. FRIDAY, JULY 7, 1899.</p>
        <p>and would wave a kiss and then the mother would feel herself more than recompensed for all hsr trouble. One striking feature of the pro- ces:qion was tl-le perfect order kept all along the route. Every detail was carried out with tIl utmos% preeisioa to the strains of be bands, and even in parts of the pro:session wher,) the music could not be heard and a temporary halt was made the little maidens and their escorts "marked time" as if by instinct. In addition to the Borough Band, the League of the Cross and Father Nugent's Flute Band also occupied places in the procession. Of cairse, the ritual of the Roman Catholics lends itself admirably to such displays,but apart from that fact it was clear that :the beautiful dresses of the scholars and the perfection of other details were chiefly due to the whole-hearted efforts on the part of the parents and priests. Flowers . played a very important part in the whole. Each school was distinguishable by some special feature Here could be seen a huge cross of roses carried by two charmingly dressed young ladies, with other dainty little maidens holding gaily-coloured though superfluous ribbons, and there a large harp. Then would come a large brass cross with the figure of Our Lord on it, followed by the statue of Our Lady. It is not surprising that the general result should be so good when each little lady was so exquisitely attired, in many cases with a garland of roses on her head and a bouquet of flowers in her hand ; often accompanied by a little cavalier, whose sober black was redeemed by a sash of vivid red or other colour as the case might be. It is estimated that there are 20,000 persons connected with Sunday Schools in the Borough, but of course, only a portkn of this number entered the Park. The greater portion of the Nonconformists left the town early in the morning for Dunham Hill, Ilelsby, Frodsham, Lymm, Southport, Apple- ton, and other placcs in the neighbourhood. Those schools that remained in town proceeded to different fields in the afternoon, and the children enjoyed themselves until evening, when the rain marred their pleasure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>T.N ItRING TON WALKING</head>
        <p>T.N ItRING TON WALKING</p>
        <p>DAY.</p>
        <p>TIIE P ROCESSIONS.</p>
        <p>On Friday Warring oe wore its annual festive ap- pearance on the occasion of the Chtldti's WAlking Day. The sun shone with all its brilliancy ; the air was filled with music ; tne dullness of the streets was relieved by the display of flags and bunting ; I but more pleasing still were the bright and happy I faces of the children rollicking in their " Sunday best." Truly this is a Children's Day, and he would , Ibe a bold man who ventured to (lex the Sunday I School scholars of this one day of real happiness I once a twelvemonth. Its approach is viewed with pleasurable anticipations by thousands of children, and:when, as was the case on Friday, the weather is propitious, no event in the town can excel it in picturesqueness and delight. To view the living wealth of colour and beauty as it traverses the streets, and which may be said to reach its climax in the pleasing ensemble before the Town Hall, is a Privilege not readily forgotten. It is true that there is much of a sameness about the event year after year but this could be obviated by a few innovations and a little variety in arranging the procession. The Roman Catholics who have for so long cultivated a taste for appealing to the eye made all display which excelled all their pre- vious efforts. From an aesthetic point of view the Protestants and Nonconformists were beaten and outdone. But still without entering into the elaborateness of the Roman Catholic procession the Protestants could by the introduction of a few new banners and other innovations add to the beauty of the spectacle. It is just a little too late to flaunt flags celebrating the Diamond Jubilee of the Queen as was the case with one school on Friday. It must have been a source of great gratificat.on to all, that beautifully fine weather favoured the festival. During the week the weather was very unsettled, and the thunderstorm of Wednesday and the threatening weather of Thursday, gave rise to fears of rain on the day of the popular fete. But such fears were dispelled by the bright sunshine which continued from early morn to late in the afternoon. At 5-30 a regrettable fall of rain was experienced. The streets,as already intimated were gaily decorated; flags and banners floated in various parts of the town, and the whole length of the line of procession was bedecked with bunting. By nine o'clock the public began to assemble along the line of the route of the procession, and when the scholars walked along the streets, the whole presented an animated appearance. The scholars commenced to arrive on the lawn in front of the Town Hall shortly before ten o'clock; and the marshalling of the schools was under- taken by the Mayor (Alderman Wright), Mr. R. T. T Fairclough, and Mr. J. Craik. Excellent I arrangements had been made by the police, and the Chief Constable and his staff are to be complimented , upon their work. The Chief Constable was the I cynosure of all eyes. With a uniform of blue and gold tipped with red and the Borough Arms em- blazoned on the epaulettes; a glittering helmet and a sword dangling at his side; he presented an appearance that might well fill the officers of many a crack regiment with envy. Two schools were late in arriving on the lawn— St. Paul's and the Padgate Industrial--and it was found impossible to let them proceed in the order which had been arranged. At a quarter past ten,the bugle sounded for the first school to move off. The Blue Coat School occupied the place of honour and headed by their own band they filed out through the central arch. The route taken was along Sankey- street, Arpley-street, Wilson-Patten-steeet, past Arpley Station, and Bridge-street, after which the schools separated for their several churches where services were held. The following was the order in which schools left the Park :</p>
        <p>1 Blue Coat School and Band 2 Parish Church and Band of Bth and 40th Regiments 3 St. George's and Walton Band 4 St. Clement's and Volunteer Bugle Band 5 Padgate Industrial Schools and Band 6 Holy Trinity and' Guardian" Band 7 Factory Yard Mission 8 St. Luke's School 9 St. Paul's and Band 10 ~11 Saints' Mission 11 St. James' and Volunteer Band 12 St. Ann's and Band 13 St. Barnabas' and Fletcher's Band 14 St. Peter's and Post Office Band 15 Emmanuel Reformed church 16 Legh-street Primitive Methodist 17 Buckley-street Methodist Free Church and Band 18 Latchford Primitive Methodist 19 Sankey Brielges Wesleyan and Monks' Band 20 Bostock-street Mission 21 Salvation Army and Band _ _</p>
        <p>22 Ragged School and Band It was originally intended that St. Paul's shnuld move off immediately after the Parish Church and Mission Schools, but owing to their late arrival their position was altered to that indicated above. We understand that the officials were in no way to blame for the delay, but that for some unaccountable is- aeon they were blocked en route. Some little dies satisfaction was also occasioned by the sandwiching of the Buckley-street school between the Legh- straet and Latchford Primitive Methodists. This was a palpable error, for surely the Primitive Methodists are entitled to walk together. A num- ber of Guardians showed their interest ,in Padgate Industrial Schools by walking in front of the children, who, bye-the-bye were very neatly and attractively dressed. Amongst the Guardians present were Mrs Clay, Messrs. William Houghton, William Hickman, T H Ashcroft, LI ilibbert,A Jolley,with Mr SSutton(assistant clerk), and Mr and airs Wright (superintendent and matron). There were few special features in con- nection with the display of the schools. The Buck- ley-street school was the most noticeable. One lorry was arched over with a wealth of ferns and under its cover were half-a-dozen little damsels at- tractively attired. On another lurry a very success- ful representation of a cricket field was given, on which a number of lads in cricketing costumee seemed to be greatly enjoying themselves. .rwo very attractive lurries were also attached to St. Peter's School, which was a very strong one numerically. The lurries bore the inscription " God Bless our School," and were gaily decorated with coloured ribbons, rosettes, and banner- ettes. In the St. James's pronession, a number of girls displayed flowers attached to small poles. Decorated mail carts were once again Ito the fore in several schools, and prettily dressed children were to be seen under the canopies of bright material. In connection with St. Peter's the Church Lads Brigade gave the head of the proces- , sion a military appearance, the lads should- eringsstheirguns in soldierly style. St. George's ion also provided two attractive lurries.On one Britannia was represented and around her were typical ropresentatives of England, Ireland, Scot- land, Wales and India. The other was of a more laughable description. The nursery tale of the Old Woman who lived in a shoe and had so many Children she didn't know what to do being graphic- ally depicted. The procession was watched from the Town Hall steps by a number of ladies and gentlemen, and Alderman Harrison occupied a seat in his phaeton which stood at the foot of the steps. The total number of children who passed through the gates was an increase on the previous year and it took a little over an hour for the whole to pass out of the park. It would be useless to deny if one wanted, that from the position they have secured through their turn-out in previous years more is expected from the Catholics than any other body. The Catholic procession, ta put it briefly, excited universal ad- miration. Headed by St. Alban's with the Borough Band playing lively marches the procession wended its way through the chief streets of the town. In- stead of adopting the way of the others and walk- ing two by two the scholars proceeded in two separate lines on either side of the street—lndian file—and the effect was pleasing. Each church was preceded by its priests accompanied by a banner and the children of reach school mere dressed differently but always in perfect taste. White dresses with mauve bands repreented one, school,and scarlet bands,violet bands,red:bands,etc. all passed before the eyes in what seemed an end- less stream as the charming little fairies rejoicing in their beauty and attire moved along accepting as their due the expressions of pleasure from the bystanders all along the route. But each little one had some special object—to find someone more im- portant than all others whose thoughtfulness and salf-sacrifice was so apparent. And then when this object was accomplished the little one would blush</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>SERIOUS OUTBREAK OF SCARLET FEVER.</p>
        <p>The Stockton Heath Parochial Committee met in St Thomas's Schoolroom on Thursday last week. Mr C W Davenport presideJ, and there were also present Messrs G Pilling, Clarke, Whitley, Mason, Pemberton, with G F Ashton (clerk), J Swinton (surveyor), and J Farring- ton (nuisance inspector).</p>
        <p>THE LIGHTING OF SHIP CANAL BRIDGES. Mr F Taylor, secr,.tary to the Warrington Corporation Gas Department wrote informing the committee that at a meeting of the Gas Committee he placed the matter of the lighting of Leatchford and other bridges before them and received instructions to give the company notice that unless the account be paid within 3.4 days the light- ing of the lamps In the district near to the bridges would be discontinued and the pillars would be taken away. In a further communication on June 16th, Mr Taylor wrote that the lamps would bt discontinued to be lighted after that night. The Chairman said the matter did not affect the Stockton Heath district</p>
        <p>THE REMOVAL OF NIGHTSOIL. Mr. Whitley stated that at the request of the com- mittee he saw Mr. Newton with respect to the termina- tion of his contract for the removal of nightsoil and Mr. Newton said he was willing to terminate it and would write the Clerk to that effect. The Clerk said he had not received ady communica- tion. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Farrington said he had had a personal interview with Mr. Newi.on who was rather surprised to hear that there were any objections to the way be carried out his contract. He was willing to take the usual notice—six months. He bad no complaints, and was quite willing to go round the township with the sub-committe for an inspection. Mr. Newton also added that he had been round in company with the surveyor. Mr. Swinton said he did visit several places in the township in company with Mr. Newton and everything seemed to be done in a satisfactory way. Mr. Mason said there had been grave reasons for com- plaint, but quite recently there had been a great im- provement. After some discussion it was decided to give Mr. New- ton six months notice for the termination of the con- tract.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>TUE PAIL SYSTEM;</head>
        <p>TUE PAIL SYSTEM;</p>
        <p>A tender was opened from Mr. Miller for the supply of pails in the event of that system being adopted by the Committee. - -</p>
        <p>It was decided to ask for other tenders. THE FLUSHING OF SEWERS.</p>
        <p>The Chairman called the attention of the Committee to tha condition of the sewers, and suggested that they be flushed periodically. Mr. Whitley proposed that the manholes be cleaned out once a quarter, and that the sewers be flushed once every six months in those districts where the Surveyor considered it necessary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>PLANS.</head>
        <p>PLANS.</p>
        <p>The Surveyor submitted a plan for a workshop tI be erected by Mr. Frank Warburton, and it was approved of.</p>
        <p>SERIOUS OUTBREAK Or SCARLET FEVER. Mr. Farrington (sanitary inspector) submitted his report, and referred to the outbreak of scarlet fever at Stockton Heath. He read out a long list of cases at present being attended to, and reported as to the steps he had token for disinfecting the houses. He complained that in some cases persons did not call in a dGcter, and no reports of the cases could therefore be obtained. Mr. Clarke: I should like to ask how many cases have been reported by the Medical Officer ? Mr. Farrington : The list of cases I have been reading came from him. The Chairman : The Medical Officer has visited a greet many since the last Board meeting at Dutton. Mr. Mason contended that there had been great re- misene;s in the matter. Scarlet fever had been raging, in the township for some time, and he did hear that there had been 70 or 80 cases. He mentioned the matter at the last meeting of the committee, but was told that there were only four or five cases. Mr. Clarke said there could be no doubt that the health of the district was in an unsatisfactory state. There was quite a number of fever cases and it was a detriment to the district and the ratepayers that such a state of Mines existed. He thought their thanks were due to Mr. Davenport for bringing the matter before the attention of the District Council. That authority ought to know that they were dissatisfied with the way Stockton Heath was being supervised. (Hear, hear.) It was not right that an epidemic of fever should he pre- vailing and little or no attention b,ing paid to it by the respossible officials. He would propose a resolution that the attention of the District Council be drawn to the unsatisfactory state of the health of Stockton Heath and that they be asked to ascertain where the fault lies and take steps to prevent a repetition of the same. Mr. Pemberton seconded the proposition. Mr. Farrington said there was a number of cases that had not been notified because a doctor had not been called in and the consequence was that no disinfection took place. Some of the children were being sent to school from infected hou3es and that caused the disease to spread. He wished them to understand that scarlet fever was not a filthy disease. It was not a questiontlof drainage but of stopping the infection. If he could get some evidence a where people had been exposing patients suffering from disease and neglecting te call in medicel man the Rural Distriet Council would be will- ing to take proceedings and snake an example of them. The Chairman said he believed the disease came from Warrington, but owing to negligence it was not dealt with effectively at the commencement. Mr. P, mberton thought they needed more official supervision at Serkton Heath than they at present got. The only way to detect offenders was to have some- one on the look-out. They did not get that supervi- sion which the importance of the place and the rateable value demanded. If there were not sufficient officials the District Council onght to provide more. The resolution on being put to the meeting, was un- animously carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>MOST DELICIOUS LEMONADE</head>
