      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <p>NEW TALE!</p>
        <p>NEW TALE!</p>
        <p>NEW TALE!</p>
        <p>IN OUR ISSUE OF FRIDAY, JULY 14TH.</p>
        <p>WE SHALL PUBLISH THE OPENING CHAPTERS OF</p>
        <p>A SPLENDID</p>
        <p>NEW SERIAL STORY,</p>
        <p>ENTITLRD</p>
        <p>" THE REVENGE OF VALERIE,"</p>
        <p>BY HUME NISBET,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>AUTHOR OF</head>
        <p>AUTHOR OF</p>
        <p>" THE SAVAGE QUEEN," "THE DIVERS," "BAIL UP," "HUNTING FOR GOLD," "LIFE AND NATURE STUDIES." "BIGHT BELLS," "DR. BERNARD ST. VINCENT." ETC.. ETC.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>SOMETHING ABOUT</head>
        <p>SOMETHING ABOUT</p>
        <p>" THE REVENGE OF VALERIE.</p>
        <p>"TUB REVENGE OF VALERIE " is an exciting novel about Klondyke which has solid foundations of fact. Among MIL NISBET'S intimate friends is an expert gold digger from Australia who was one of the pioneers of vid-mining in British Columbia. and has kept in touch with the most stirring events and even the smaller details of existence out there, which both of them from their earlier Austral experi-</p>
        <p>ences are able to appreciate as no ordinary observer could. The love theme of the story is strong and the characters are drawn with skill and strength,induced by a study of human nature in many lands. Sir Lewis Eastlake is introduced to the reader, when, at twenty-four years of age, he is going the pace in a London Gambling Club. An altercation there causes a duel at Cherbourg in which his opponent is killed. Sir Lewis has been accompanied by his Club com- panion, Captain Eric St. Arnoul,who has him nursed at the house of a Cherbourg young doctor,an acquaint- ance of some years, wigs a pretty sister who does the nursing. The story, however, centres on the passion of a woman for a man rather than on that of a man for a woman, The beautiful and wealthy Duchess Valerie has from motives of affectiou been finding Sir Lewis Eastlake with money through a financier, and when she learns of his illness, she arranges with her cousin, Captain St. Arnoul, for the invalid's removal to her seat at Calais.the Captain to be his escort and companion. She thinks that now it will all end well. little imagining that Sir Lewis has before the duel been privately married to the doctor's sister, his engagement to her having been of some standing. Sir Lewis on his recovery goes alone to seek a fortune for himself and wife at Kiondyke, and while he is encountering adventures there, the Duchess, on a</p>
        <p>reaction of sentiment for him, cruelly plots to ruin the character of his wife, and so bring about a divorce to be followed by her own marriage to bin. The Duchess attains her triumph, but the girl has a counter revenge which makes a striking climax.</p>
        <p>NOTE THE FACT! that this absorbingly interesting tale by a popular author, commences on</p>
        <p>J ULY 14T11. JULY 14T11.</p>
        <p>JULY 14111.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>trtitto to tbe Otittor.</head>
        <p>trtitto to tbe Otittor.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in at cases send their names and addresses to the Editor. Matters intenthd for publication must be written on one side only of the paper. Correspondents are s.rongly advised to write briefly. A short letter has much greater chance of insertion than a long one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>OUR PUBLIC BATHS.</head>
        <p>OUR PUBLIC BATHS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am astouisLed to find what a great grievance exists amongst workingmen who cannot get a private bath in our public baths without paying the extravagant sum of sixpence. Now everyoae will admit that for cleanliness and health it is absolutely necessary that a workingman should have at least one bath a week. But the question is tutted how can a man with a large family and a small wage afford to pay this sum ? And it is further asked why as in Notthwich and other places a three- penny bath cannot be ptovided ? I must confess Sir, that when I heard of the sixpenny charge I was some- what surprised, because lan sure a bath for the lower rate migot be provided, and with the extra nnmber of applicants at threepence, it would pay the committee better than the sixpenny one all round. At any rate I do think that in the case of private baths, that if it is not convenient to have three penny baths all round,and a distinction must be made, a number should be al- lowed at the former sum. To me. it is net so much a matter of paying, as it is to encourage cleanliness, and in order to convenience a large number of working men. The question is of such importance, that it is well worth considering. S. D.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>SUNDAY TRADING.</head>
        <p>SUNDAY TRADING.</p>
        <p>Sir—While thoroughly agreeing ith your corrosion dent " Iona" and the Rev. Vicar of St. Paul's as to the necessity of a day of rest for weekly workers' whether sian or beast, I think the method by which so desirable an object is sought to be obtained is unwise and unsound. I think it an outrage on common Sense, logic and arithereetic to try to persuade the workingman that Su- nday is the seventh day which was sanctified as the day. of rest,when if he comes to note it arithmetically he does thus—l day 9-VII-1899. To solemnly tell a man that it is a sin to buy his children a pennyworth of lollipops on a Sunday and to recommend him to do this on the seventh, which any Jew or any Quaker will til him was the day appointed to be sanctified for rest—is to give the whole case away by making it ridiculous. The eontirual crying of" Wolf ! Wolf ! "like this over mere peccadilloes is the way to minimise one's sense of g eater faults. Ellicott= has now reached such an extsnt that the workingman sees for hiinielf that Suuday is no-where mentioned in the Scriptures, and no interdict therein contained against working or trading en our first day or Sunday. Up to the time of Constantine our Saterday was the Day ..of Rest—that being the seventh, but he, to suit his own purposes, altered it to Sunday— be being a Sun Worshipper. Surely so beneficial an ob- servance might be advocated ou its own merits without resorting to prevarication.—Truly yours. JOEIN PEERS. Pecketh, July 3, 1899.</p>
        <p>SUNDAY TRADING. Sir,—On the authority of " .fhe Manchester Guar- dian" of this day I learn that Mr. Robert Pierpoint, member for Warrington, had informed our Town Coun- cil that their all, in a somewhat mutilated form it is true, had passed the third reading in the House of Com- mons. The information must have proved very agree- able to the aldermen and councillors, and their trusty henchmen, the Corporation officials, who laboured so hard and so disinterestedly for the promotion of the scheme. The news, too, seems to have given a fresh stimulus to cur local rulers to proceed further in the path of beneficent legislation. For your contemporary tells us that the Council, at their meeting on the 4th inst., confirmed a scheme formulated by the Water Committee, whereby the milkman will be prevented from going his rounds after 10 a.m., on Sunday, and the voice ot the newsboy will b 3 heard no more in our atreets on that day. No sensible person will object to these little reforms. But the pettifosging policy which swallows the whole camel of open public houses and carriage traffic on the Sabbath Day, and strains at the gnat of the poor milkman and newsboy will not do much to es- tablish the reputation of our ceuncillors as Wise and righteous men. Surely when evils are to be dealt with common sense would dictate that the larger ones should be attended to first, leaving the small and less pressing ones to gradually right themselves. Whatever wrong the sellers of milk and newspapers may do to the Day of 'Rest it is assuredly small in c-mparison with the whole- sale oesecritirm of that sacred day by the liquor and carriage traffickers whese aim seems to be to deprive us of the Sabbath altogether.—Yours truly, 5 July, 1899</p>
        <p>lONA.</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S Coco), is absolutely pure, being entirely free from kola, malt, hops, alkali, or emy foreign admixture C‘ution I The public should insist on having CADBURY'S —sold only in Packets andains—as other Cocoas are often 4ubstituted for the rake of extra profit.—Advt. (4).</p>
        <p>CHOICE DAIRY FED PORK, fresh daily. J. BiItLEY ISAAC, Hors op ark et-street. —Advt. The ttroprietors of the Royal Oak Soap Works tender their apologies to their paErons f,ir any inc,inveniense that may have been felt in getting their Coupons. for tree Tablets of Britannia Herb So sp exchangad. This has been due to the excessive call for these TablAs sad the makers beg to intimate that they have now very considerably increased their plant in this department to cope with the growing demand so that no further delay will be experiential. They ake this opportunity of mentioning that they have now introduced a best Pink Carbolic Soap under the brand " Giant Oak" with which the free tablets of Herb Soap are Eliven same as with the Royal Oak. Everybody should use these high class soaps,—Advt. CYCLES. Premiers. £lO 10s; Excelsior'. £l2 Os Od New Rapids £l5 10s ad. Sole Agent J. S. GARLAIM Old Established, Honest Dealers, SCALES AND SONS, 83, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. POTTED TONGUE, PRESSED BEEF, POTTED HEAD, BOILED HAMS. all kinds of cooked meats.</p>
