      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JULY 7, 199</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JULY 7, 199</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.INEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL. The ordinary monthly meeting of this council was held in the Town Hall, Earlestown, on Monday evening. Mr. CB F Borron presided, anal there were als present : Messrs. J W Emmett J N Siddeley, %V Gihnour, F J Wood, J Simpson, J N Patterson, W Tuompson, E T Evans, T Ormand, with Mr W W Shirley (clerk), Mr Brierley (sanitary inspector), Mr Bowes (surveyor) and Mr Lloyd.(gas engineeer). CEMETERY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>POLICE CHANGES.</p>
        <p>P.C. Aspder. vho has been -stationed at Earles- town for the pat 2 or 3 years has been transferred to the Burnley Division, and the vacancy has been tilled by the tran-ler of P.O Lennie trom Burnley• VIADUCT INSTITUTE TECHNICAL INSTAUCTION CLASSES.</p>
        <p>The result of the e-x-a-Min-a-tion in mathematics held in connection with the above classes is as fol- lows :—Giles Colling, stage 2, 2nd ; A R Hill, F J Robinson, H Williamson, Stage 1, Ist; W H Beilby, Lilly Brownlee, J A Bullock, C L Haynes, E D Lyons, stage 1, 2nd. SHOCKING. FATALITY AT GrOLBORNE. . _</p>
        <p>This committee ordered that tenders for the growing crop of grass at the Cemetery be invited. The success- ful tenderer to cut and remove same at his own cost. The Chairman and Mr. Ormand to accept such tender as they may: consider moat advantageous. The minutes were approved en the motion cf Mr. Simpson. THE TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION GRANT. The Technical Instruction Committee's minutes stated that the clerk report° i the receipt of a cheque of £BO 10s. from the Lancashire County Council, being the final instalment of the grant for Technical Instruction purposes for this district for the Session, 1898-99. It was resolved that the Finance Committee be recom- mended t 3 pass an account in favour of the Viaduct Institute Technical Instruction Committee for £BO Dr. Patterson moved the confirmation of the minutes which were approved. GAS AND WATER COMMITTEE. THE TEST BOREHOLE.</p>
        <p>Ella Davies, aged sixteen months, of Homes Inn, Golborne, the daughter of a collier, has met her death under sad circumstances. The mother of the deceased had just prepared a can of hot tea for her father, when the child upset the tea over her chest, ana sustained shocking scalds, to which she suc- cumbed. This is the second fatality within a week under precisely similar circumstances. THE NEW POLICE BUILDINGS AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>The new police buildings in course of erection at Newton, are rapidly nearing completion, and it is expected they will be ready for occupation in November next. It is intended to hold the usual Petty Sessions in the new buildings, instead of at the Newton Town Hall as at present. There will also be residence for a sergeant, but whether the Inspector will be transferred there or not, is not at present clear. THE DROWNING CASE AT EA.RLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Gas and Water Committee, the Chairman reported that the Gasworks Extension Com- mittee had had before them a letter from the Vulcan Foundry, Limited, applying for a supply of gas to their works and cottages, and that they (the sub-Committee) bad approved of a plan showing the position of proposed mains with an estimate of the cost thereof, and recom- mended the Gas and Water Committee to accede to the application, whereupon it was resolved that the applica- tion be acceded to, subject to the Vulcan Foundry enter- ing into an agreement giving the Council free access to the mains for repairs end inspection ; and in the event of necessity arising for their removal, the Company to refund the Council the whole cost of laying such mains. It was further resolved that the Comet berecemmended to withdraw the plan suhmitted to the Local Govern- ment Board, with the application for sanction te borrow the sum of £3,050 for the extensions of gas mains, and that the plan now submitted showing the proposed ex- tension to Messrs. McCorquoiale's Works and the Vulcan Foundry, Limited, be adopted. The gas and water engineer reported that by the adjust- ment of the valves and dividing the district into two sections a greater and a more c qualised pressure of water throughout the district had been obtained. The Gas Works Extensions Sub Committee reported :—(a) That they had accepted the tender of the Earl of Bradford of 14s 3d per ton of cannel free on rails Earlestown Station; (b) the tender-of Messrs. R. Evans and Co. of 6s 6d per ten of Rushy Park slack, and (c) the ten ler of Messrs. Wright and Co., London, for a photometer and neces- sary accessories for the sum of £Bl 5i 61. A letter was read from Mr. J. Bridge,of Parkside, Newton-le-Willows, stating that he was inconvenienced in coniequence of his not having a supply of water on his farm premises, and requesting to be supplied with water with as little delay as possible. The Chairman reported that the Clerk had already intimated to Mr. Bridge that he could be supplied with water on the same terms and conditions offered him two years ago. The Chairman in moving the confirmation of the minutes, said the test borehole had been pumping water forisonoe time and they were getting such a supply as would meet the present requirements of the township. As regarded the quality of the water they had sent it to be tested and he hoped at the next committee meeting to lay the full facts before them. Mr. Gilmour said the Chairman bad stated that they had got a sufficient supply. He wanted to know what was the supply at present. It was a very vague ex- pression to say they had a sufficient supply for they bad got the impression that they were going to have an abundance of water when they had sunk the borehole. The pressure in the higher parts of the township was now higher than they had had for years. He thought the Chairman's remarks were misleading. The Chairman said his observation was that the test borehole was yielding a quantity of water equal to their present requirements. As he had already said,he hoped to lay before thelcommittee Mr. Gaskell's report and the analyst's report with full particulars at the next meeting. Mr. Emmett asked the question whether the pumps put down were of a permanent or only a temporary character.! The Chairman said he took it they were only tempor- ary. Mr. Gilmour sail it would have been better for the Chairman not to introduce the subject until be knew. The Chairman said he would not, trouble them with any further information. If Mr. Gilmour thought his remarks were not for the information of the Council and the public he hoped he would not continue the discus- son. Mr. Emmett said the reason he asked the question was that a short time ago the Chairman gave him the impression that permanent pumps were being put down. Mr. Gilmour remarked that he could have given the Chairman acme information. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Emmett said he did not wish to hear it. The Chairman said he was sorry if any expression of his could have misled any inemb9rs of the Council. He did not think the Committee had given the Sub-Com- mittee authority to lay down permanent pumps at the bore-hole. This was a mere test borehole, and it was for the Committee te say what they would do after- wards. The minutes were then passed. HIGHWAYS AND SANITARY COMMITTEE. MR. GILMOUR IN A VIGILANT MOOD.</p>
        <p>The inquest on the boy Harry Cronin, who was drowned under circumstances reported in our last issue was held at the Town Hall, Earlestown on Monday evening by Mr. F. A. Jones,deputy coroner. Evidence bearing out the facts already stated was given by Edward Cronin, the father, Walter All- dridge. the boy who was with the deceased, and Jackson, who endeavoured to rescue hins,and a ver- dict of "Accidently drowned" was returned. 1.0.G.T. HOPE OF EARLBSTOWN LODGE, NO. 1644. The weekly meeting was held in the Co-operative Hall, Wargrave-road, on Monday, Bro Williams C.T. in the chair. After the opening services, one mem- ber was restored and one was initiated. It was proposed that the picnic stand over till after the Bank Holiday. Notice of resolution was given to hold open air meetings. The programme was then proceeded with: Song, Sister Grant, "Tis but a little faded flower :" recitation, Bro Stanley; imita- tion of banjo solo, Bro Corney ;"Gough'.l oration on water," Bro Jones; mouth organ solo, Bro Wilson; song, Sister Williams, "Grandma sitting in the old arm chair "; sacred song, Bro Mockling "Bless His lholy name:' Bro Corney gave an amusing ventrilo- quial and prhenological exhibition, the ledge being I closed by Bro Williams C.T. at 9-30.</p>
        <p>The Highways and Sanitary Committee recommended that the following building plans be approved and adopted :—Rev. W. O'Reilly, extensions Co dwelling- house ; Mr. J. Moon. Warrington, nine cettages,Cherry- street ; Messrs. R. Evans and Company, plan showing proposed streets. The report of the Sub-Committee appointed to consider the application of the roads work- men for an advance of wages was read, after which it was resolved that the wages of Messrs. Poulsen, Spell- man, and Eaton be increased is. each per week, and Glynn 21. per week. The sub-committee appointed to consider the question of the Surveyor's application for clerical assistance reported that they could not recom- mend the Council to augment the staff in the Sur- veyor's department at the present time, and that the matter be deferred for say six months. The Surveyor, as instructed, reported as to the repair of Swan-road. Newton Common, and it was afterwards re- solved that the Chairman, with the Clerk and Surveyor, approach the Haydock District Council and endeavour to make arrangements whereby the road leading from the Swan Inn to Haydock could be opened as a public road betweon Earlestewn and Haydock. It was ale, re- eelved that the tender of Mr. W. Longten, of Fair- brother's Farm, Newton-le-Willows, for carting and team labour as may be required by this com- mittee for one year from the Ist of July, 1899, to June 30th, 1900, according to the schedule of prices named in his tender be accepted. The Clerk reported the result of his interview with Mr. Pardey, with reference to a nightsoil depot on land west of Vista-road, in the occupation of Mr. J. Berry, and stated that Mr. Parley had agreed not to raise objec- tions to the proposed site, subject to the Council obtain- ing the consent of the tenant, and making a suitable road, and both road and site to be fenced off ; all loose paper to be burned or otherwise destroyed ; drinking ponds or pits (if any), to be made secure from pollution; and (if necessary), the site to be drained. The Clerk further reported that he had also interviewed Mr. Berry the tenant of the land referred to, and that Mr. Berry declined to treat the nightsoil himself, but would be quite willing to sublet the Council a sufficient area of lard for treatment of the nightsoil by themselves. After consideration, it was resolved that Mr. Pardey be re- quested to lease to the Council, for nye or seven years, a sufficient area of land for the purpose of the Council depositing and treating the nightsoil themselves on the site referred to, and after treatm-nt, to be sold as manure, at a cost per load to be hereafter determined upon. It was also resolved that T. Bush be instructed to assist the sanitary departi-neet until the scarlet fever and measles cases had again become normal.—The re- port of the sub-Committee, having reference to the cost of removal of nightsoil, was read, from which it appeared that they had instructed Mr. Gregory to report to the full committee upon some scheme whereby the cost could be reduced without impairing the efficiency of the werk.—Mr. Gregory then reported that he had given the matter careful consideration, but was unable to suggest any scheme for the reduction of the cost other than having the nightsoil removed by contract, and which he could not recommend. —After cilscussion thereon, it was crdered that the matter be deferred to the n xt ordinary meeting of the Committee, meanwhile the Clerk to write to a number of othrr local authori- ties for information upon the subject, and lay before the Committee.—A leter was read from the Medical Officer of Health, referring to the inability of the Warrington Corporation, to admit at the present time in their bola- don Hospital. infectious cases from this district, and that he was keeping himself in constant communication with their Medical Officer, Dr. tiornall.—A letter dated the 27th May. 1899, was read from the Local Govern- ment Board adverting to the annual report of the Medi- cal Officer of Health for this district for the year 1898, and desiring to be furnished with the Council's observa- tions on certain paragraphs in the report referred to in the letter. Mr. Gilmour said in his opinion the minutes were not a correct record. He referred to the paragraph respect- ing the acceptance of Mr. Lungton's tender for carting and team labour. In this • instance there was a motion</p>
        <p>and an amendment and he contended that it ought be so stated in the minute. It was not agreed to un- animously and the minutes was not a correct record. The Chairman : May I ask you to propose an amend- ment.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gilmour : It is not a proper record and I say it ought to be deleted and put in a better fern. The Clerk submitted that the minute was strictly regular and in proper form. It said " resolved " and it was the record nf the committee to the Council. Had the meeting been a Council meeting when the tender was accepted it would have been necessary to record the proposer and seconder of the resolution as well as the proposer and seconder of the amendment,as well as the result of the voting. With regard to Mr. Gilmour's sontention—.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gilmour : I object moat strongly to any remark being made personal.</p>
        <p>The Chairmin : I will call the Clerk to order when I think he is out of order.</p>
        <p>The Clerk (continuin) said he had no doubt that the opinion quoted from a Liverpool paper referred exclu- sively to proceedings in Council and not when in com- mittee. The resolution in question was passed at a coin- mittee meeting and if there was only a majority in favour of it still it wes the resolution of the committee.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gilmour said anyone looking at the minute would think there was only one tender considered. Fie had hail the opinion from a Liverpool editor, and from councillors and magistrates that the proceedings in com- mittee should be recorded, and the amendments given. The Chairman said he was sorry to say that magis- trates and councillors were not infallible. It was for the Council to say whether they considered the minutes an adequate record of the committee's proceedings. Mr. Gilmour: The resolution is misleading.</p>
        <p>The Chairman then put the [minutes tor the confirma- tion of the Council and they were passed, Mr. Gilmour onlyidissentinz.</p>
        <p>GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>THE RAILWAY ALTERA !lONS. -SATISFACTORY REPLY.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee the Chairman made a statement as to the position of the Light Railways Committee of the Council and the Lan- cashire Light Railways Company, and read a copy of a letter which Messrs. Peace and Ellis had received from Messrs. Leslie and Co., solicitors, stating that they (the Light Railways Company) must leave them (the Coun- cil) to take their own course,after which it was resolved that the Draft Order of the Lancashire Light Railway Co. be opposed before the Light Railway Commissioners, and if necessary before the Board of Trade, unless the terms laid down by the sub-Committee in the draft I agreement are agri7ed to by the company, and that Messrs. Peace and Ellis, solicitors, Wigan, be and they are hereby appointed and instructed tu conduct such op- position. A letter was read from Mr. J. Shaw, district superintendent of the London and North-Western Rail- way Co., stating that the train alterations for the sum- mer were now settied,therefore nothing further could be done just now with regard to additional stopping trains, but that he would keep the Council's letter before him when next considering train alterations. The question of a new or increased passenger station accommodation at Earlestown was again brought forward,and the Clerk mentioned that it would be two years in August next since the matter was first taken up with the London and North-Western Railway Conspany,and although repeated letters had passed ta and fro no progress whatever was apparent. It was thereupon ordered that the Clerk communicate directly with the Right Hon. Lord Stal- bridge, chairman of the L. and N,-W. Railway Co., and advert to the correspondence which had already taken place, and urge upon his lordship the necessity of some progress being made without further delay, and that a copy of the letter be forwarded to Mr. F. Harri- son, general manager of the Company. The question of the delay with reference to the enlargement of Earles- town Post Office was also brought forward, and it was ordered that the Clerk draw the attention of the Post- master-General to this matter.</p>
        <p>The Chairman moved the confirmation of the minutes, and said he had received the f4llowinx reply from Lord Sta'bridge : "I have received your letter of the 26th June and given instructions for piers to be prepared. You may be sure there will be no unnecessary delay in effecting the alterations which we contemplate." The reply was considered very satisfactory. Mr. G.lmour : What reply has been received from the Postmaster-General ?</p>
        <p>The Clerk : No reply has yet been received. The minutes were passed and the Council then rose.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF OUA.RDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Guardians was held at Dutton Workhouse, on Tuesday. There were present:— Mr. J. Mosley Williams (chairman), Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Messrs. S. Taylor, A. R. Norman, T. Lea. T. Reynolds, the members of the Rural District Council, with Mr. G. F. Ashton (clerk), and Mr. W. E. Hough (assistant clerk):</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>SCHOLASTIC APPOINTMENT AT ST. HELENS. Mr. Gibb, a master at St. Nicholas's Church Schools Liverpool, was on Tuesday night appointed head master of St. Thomas's Schools, St. Helens, in succession to the late Mr. J. Rudge. The Rev. L. J. Rich, formerly of St. Nioholaa's, is the vicar of St. Thomas's.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>i HUSBAND'S MAGNANIMITY.</head>
        <p>i HUSBAND'S MAGNANIMITY.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens, on Monday. John Gallagher. an elderly man, was charged on remand with disobeying an order of the justices to contribuze 63. per week to his wife,and being £9 193. in arrear. Gallagher was charged last Friday, when he said that during the time his wife had been away he bad put the money in the Post Office Sav- ings Bank. He then offered to get the money out of the Post Office and give it to her. Ga,lagher now appeared with the inoney,which he paid over with great cheerful- mess, the people in court appearing absolutely dumb- founded at the fact that he was willing to part with so much money. Gallagher endeavoured to speak to his wife, apparently being wishful to make it up with her</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>EARLESTOWN, NEWTON A. ND DISTRICT</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN, NEWTON A. ND DISTRICT</p>
        <p>FAN CIERS ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The annual exhibition of first feather birds in connec- tion with the above Association was held in the Club room of the Oddfellows Arms, Earlestown, on Friday and Saturday last. The number of entries was quite up to the average of previous years, and the quality of the birds shown was very good, the exhibitors being chieEy from the district. The exhibita were staged on each side of the club room, and they were inspected by a fairly large number of visitors. The judges were Mr. John Williams, of Liverpool, for cage birds, and Mr. John WebEter, of Chorley, for pigeons, and their awards were as follow :</p>
        <p>Clear Yellow Yorkshire: 1, W Bray ; 2, Eeyes ;3, Joseph Smith zv hc and h c, Joseph Smith. Clear Buff Yorkshire: 1 and special for best bird in show, and medal, W Bray ; 2, J Appleton ; 3 and v h c, Joseph Smith. Ticked Yellow Yorkshire: 1, W Bray ; 2, Caldwell ; 3, Joseph Smith; v h c, Heyes. Tickei Buff Yorkshire : 1, W Bray; 2 and 3,and v h c, Joseph Smith. Clear Buff Norwich: 1, J Appleton; 2, James Smith ; 3 Heyea. Selling Class, W Bray. Foreign Birds clam : 1, Caldwell with a Madagascar love bird; 2, and 3, and vhcJ Appleton Pieeons, Working Homer Cock: 1, J Wightman, 2 and 3,T Lythgoe ; 4, c,J Wightman; v h c,T Hallett; h c, W Duttfield; c T Lythgoe. Working Homer hen, 1 and 2. J Wightman ; 3 T Lythgoe ; v h e, W Duttfield ; la c, F Hallett.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>RECKLESS CONDIJC-V BY MINERS AT BURTON WOOD.</head>
        <p>RECKLESS CONDIJC-V BY MINERS AT BURTON WOOD.</p>
        <p>Several important CaVS were heard at the Warring- ton County Petty Sessions, on Wednesday held in the Registrar's Court at the County Court. before Mr. Comber (in the chair) and other magistrates. The first case was against Thomas Haverty, a miner, who was charged with having on the 20th June, at Burtonwooi, while in the employ of the Collins Green Colliery Com- pany, Limited, acted in contravention of the Coal Mines Regulation Act,lBB7.by having loose in his possession four ounces of carbonite.—Mr. Browne appeared to Proe- cute on behalf of the company, and stated that the car- bonito was found loose in the defendant's pocket. These proceedings were taken for the safety of the men, and if they would persist through simple laziness and neglect in going into the mines with loose explosives in their peckets, they must take the consequences. The maxi- mum penalty was 40s. and costs. The inspecter had insisted upon these proceedings being taken for the protection of the men; and, in accordance with the instructions of the !nanager, the miners were searched on the day in question, with the result named. A pal- try penalty of ss. or 103. would not suffice in this case. —Mr. Crosfield : D s they use these explosives for obtain- ing coal ? Do they blast with them P—Mr. Browne re- plied in the affirmative, and a fine of 20s and costs was intlicted.—Nathan Taylor was charged with a similar offence.—Mr. Bro aerie said that in this case the defen- dant had 16 ounces of loose carbonite in his jacket pocket. The practice was a most dangerous one.—This defendant was also fined 20s and costs.—Murdock Nicholson was charged with workicg in the pit with a lucifer match in one of his pockets.—ln this case Mr. Browne informed the Bench that a periodical examination was made on the day in question, and the match was found in the defendant's pocket. Nothing could be more dangerous than such a practice. The Miners Federation always supported these men when they were in the r ght, but in cases like the present they never did, showing that they had no sympathy with their conduct.—A. fine of 255. land costs. was intlic!ed, defend sot not having appeared in answer to the summons.</p>
        <p>BROOXE, BOND AND CO., LIMITED.—The Directors have issued their report and bilance sheet for the fin- ancial year ended 31st May last, showing that a net profit, after charging Directors' fees,Debenture interest and all other charges, amounts to £30,347, and with £4,170 hrousfht forward from last Account, there is a SUER of £34,517 to the credit of the Profit and Loss account. The report states that the usual fourteen de- bentures have been redeem,d, and the remaining 172 have been surrendered and cancelled, and the property has been r.,-coriveyed to the Company. The Direc:ors recommend a dividena at the usual rate of fifteen per cent per annum, free of Income Tax. They propose to carry £5,000 to the Reserve Fund, to write off £lO,OOO fri.m the Goodwill Account, and to carry forward a bilance of -22,333 to nexG year's account. Wi;h the additions now propised. th Reserve Fund will ho £37,000, and ciurinq the seven years the sum of £74.000 has been written off the Goodwill Accmint,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>BLIND CHILDREN'S HOLIDAY.</head>
        <p>THE BLIND CHILDREN'S HOLIDAY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Taylor, superintendent of the school for the indigent blind, Flardman-street, Liverpool, wrote as follows :—As the time for our midsummer holiday which is generady about five weeks, is fast approaching, I shall consider it a favour if you will kindly grant abou t lOs per week, for the purpose of boarding out Richard Waring, chargeable to the Runcorn Union, or sending him to a Convalescent Home. MI pupils are compelled to leave the school during the holiday, and expenses wid be charzfd in first account. Oa t' • m tiol of Mr. A. R. Norman, seconded by Mr. Mclntyre, it was declded to make the allowance asked. THE WORKHOUSE TO BE CJNNECTED WITH CIVILISATION.</p>
        <p>The Clerk stated that he had had some conversation with Mr. Charles E. Linaker, jun., about the telephone. He said they were proposing to set up a telephone centre and he wished to know whether the Union would de- cide to have the Workhouse attached. He (the Clerk) suegested that Mr. Linaker would send an official le.ter, and he cons.quently wrote stating that he had heard from the district manager re the telephone coanection, and providing they got sufficient suep )rt to open up Aston as an imaginary cen-re the rates pr annum would be :—Workhouse, £lO ; clerk's office, £3 10 ; medical officer's house, Frodsham, 10s. On a vote being taken the majority of the Board appeared to be in favour of the whole scheme. Mr. Woolward, therefore, gave notice that he would move a resolutioe that the Clerk's office,and the Medical Officer's house at Frodsham should be conn,cted by tele- phone with the Workhouse. TIIR SIGAIRMENI Or BOARDS OF GUARDIANS AND TDB</p>
        <p>MAGISTERIAL BENCH.</p>
        <p>The North Brierley Uiioa frwrdd cloy of a Bill introduced into the Rause of 031111210 M in f amour of the appointment of chairmen of Bards of Guirdiani as ex-officio Justices of the t'eace, and inviting the Runeoru Guardians to use their utmost endeavnirs to prevail upon their party representatives to d.) their ut,inost for secur- ing the passing of tha 8,11. Mr. Woodward moved that the Board support the pe- tition.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Clarke seconded, adding for instance, it would give hirn:great pleAsure to see Mr. Williams on the Bench. The motion was agreed tn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>TREAT FOR THE INMATES.</head>
        <p>TREAT FOR THE INMATES.</p>
        <p>Lady Broke wrote inviting the aged women and children to spend an afternoon at Norton Priory as they did last year. She was unable to fix a day at present as her mevements were somewhat uncertain. Should the Guardians .give permission the precise date might be settled with Mr. and Mrs. Connell to suit their conveni- ence. The Guardi ins unanimously consented.</p>
        <p>THE AUDIT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Walter Yonng,aasistant district anditor,for warded notice of ail au-lit of the acclunt of the Union and of the snreral parishes therein to be held on the 24th inst. SELEDDING ACCOMMODALTION.—ANOTIIER £l3O.</p>
        <p>Tne Local Governineiat Board wrote assenting to the proposal of the Guardians to erect a shed for accommo- dation of conveyances at the Workhlll9P noon which the Guardians proposed to soeud a sum of £l3O. The beard considered that the loan should be repaid within a period of 20 years. " FINANCES. -£lO3l ODD IN A DAY !"</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward repined that the ,smounts aranted in out-relief were :—Runcorn district £BO ; Frodsham £22; Budworth £22. The total payments that day amounted to £lOBl 164. 61,, and after that the balancs in the treasurer's hands was £2144 15 7d. Mr. Woodward drew attention to the increased amount paid in vaceina- tion fees under the new act. There was a bill from Dr. McDougall for Runcorn amounting to £23 4s. 61. The fees were some:hing more than doub'e what they used to he. Thl report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>TRINITY WESLEYAN BAND OF EIOPE.—The members of the above 134nd of flop with their friends, numbering about 80,participatedlin an enjoyable picnic to Frodsham on Saturday last. They left Halton at about one o'clock in four conveyances. After an enjoyably spent after- noon, tea was partaken of at Mersey view. The party which was accotnpanied by Mr. Looker, the superinten- dent, returned home shortly after nine Itt good spirits.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>WARRINGToN MARKETS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGToN MARKETS.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Corn Market on Wedne4day new wheat (white) fetched 43 Od (701 b. to the bu,hel) ; red wheat, 3s lid ; new oats, 2s 61 Flour best seconds, per 2401 b., 183 Cd. Bran, lid t 3 is lid per 20Ib. Prices at the Provision Market were :—Fresh butter is to is 2d per lb ; fresh eggs, 11 and 12 for is; fowls, 45 Gd to 5s 6d per couple; English beef, 5d to 101 ; lamb, 8d to is mutton 6d to 10d ; veal, 8d to 10d; pork, 6d to 8i</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <p>THE WARRINGTON EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>GENERAL PRINTING DEPARTMENT ENVEI,OP.ES 2s. PR 1,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above Council was held in the Town Hall, Runcorn. on Thursday morning,when there were present :—Messrs F Wiswall (chairman), Samuel Taylor (vice-chairman), E Lnison, W Handley, D Bisbrown, James Pritchard, T Dean, G Hewitt, E Gatley, J Wilson, M J Sweetman, H J Grice, S Walton, T Jones, R H Posnett, W Collier, with F A Lake (clerk), E Marshall (secretary), and James Wilding (surveyor), and Dr McDJugall (medical officer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>NEW MEMBER.</head>
        <p>NEW MEMBER.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. H. Posnett, t3ok his seat for the fisrt time as the representative of Heath Ward, lately filled by Mr. P. Speakman.—The Chairman observed he was sure they would all jAn him in welcoming Mr PosLett to the Board. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The number of deaths registered during the past month amounted t.) 16, two were inquest cases. The sixteen dew:hs show an average deatbirate for the:twelve months of 10,16, and exclusive of the inquest eases, of 9.3 of the estimated population. Fifty-one births were regisered and for the correiponding months of last year twenty-two deaths and 51 births were registered.</p>
        <p>THE PORT SANITARY AUTHORITY AND INFECHOUS DISEASE ON VESSELS.</p>
        <p>The Health Committee reported the receipt of the fol- lowing letter from the Manchester Port Sanitary Authority. Monks Hall, Eccles, May 20th, 1899. Mr. E. Marsha% Runcorn Urban Authority. Dear Sir,—As mentioned in my clnversation with you yesterday the above Authority as constituted at present are willing for a time to contribute the expenses, estimated at between £6OO and £7OO for 1899,provided your Authority will bea the expense of any case of infectious disease removedr from a vessel bind for or whilst within your district As an annual sum will be paid to Liverpool for the re moval and treatment of any case of plague, cholera, or yellow fever discovered there you are not asked to con tribute to this. SmaPpax will be removed at Liverpoo• at a charge of four guineas per week per case—if ycli likl these cases can come en to your hospital, but it would be better to have them removed at Liverpool. The work done for your district by the Port Sanitary Authority I mentioned to you yesterday. The Local Government Board may grant the Order for three or five years, at present it is uncertain.—Believe me, yours faithfully, J. H. CROCKER.</p>
        <p>The committee recommended that the offer be ac- cepted subject to the terms " bound for or whilst within your district" being held to mean " for the purpose of loading or Gischarging cargo." The committee further recommended that the Medical Officer of Health he paid a retaining fee of ten guineas per annum,and lOs d for each visit to a patient. and 59. for each patient over and above the first case when one or more are in the hospital in respect of cases removed from any ship to the Isolation Hospital under the fore- going agreement. The drugs to be supplied by the Council. Such arrangement to date from the comple- tion of the agreement with the Manchester Port Sani- tary Authority ; algo,tnat the secretary be authorised to open negotiations with the Liverpßol Port Sanitary Authority with a view to obtaining their con- sent to deal at Liverpool with cases of small- pox found on vessels bound for Runcern ; and that a claim received by the Committee to pay the sum of LES 8s 64, for out of pocket expenses incurred through illness alleged to be caused by bad smells arising from night soil boat at Top Locks be not entertained ; that the wage of William Carman, engaged in the disin- fecting department, be increased from 18s to £1 per week.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Dean proposed the adoption of the minutes with a slight alteration. Mr. George Hewitt seconded. Mr. D. Bisbrown said he could not vote for the pass- ing of the minutes, and moved that the minute respect- ing the: claim for "£6 8s 63 for out of pocket expenses incurred through illness alleged to be caused by bad smells arising from night soil boats at Top Locks "be referred back to the committee. He considered that Mr. Cooke had both a legal and moral claim upon them. Hu was sorry to oppose his colleagues in the matter, and would have done so in committee but that he had been unable to get down before the business was disposed of. On a vote being taken the minutes were adopted with a single dissentient (Mr. Bisbrown). ELECTRIC LIGHTING.</p>
        <p>The Parliamentary and General Purposes Committee reported that the Royal Assent had been given tr. the Bill confirming the Runcorn Electric Lighting Provisional Order. Mr. W. Handley moved the adoption of the minutes which were more in the way of an announcement. The Electric Lighting Bill was now law, and the next ques- tion was, what were they going to do with it ? Mr. J. Wil,on seconded, and proposed that the mat ter be referred to the Highways and Buildings Commit- tee with instructions to bring in a scheme in accordance with the order, and bring it before the Council. Mr. Handley observed that the Highways and Build- ings Committee could have it, as the Parliamentary Com- mittee ha-1 done with it. Mr. Imison said be should like a definition of" bring- ing in a scheme," as it seemed to imply the performance of w.rks by themselves. Did it bind them in any way? The Law Clerk suggested that the matter be simply referred to the committee to report on. Mr. Wilson said all he was anxious about was that the thing should go on. Mr. Handley then incorporated Mr. Wilson's sugges- tion, and moved the adoption of the minutes, and that the matter be referred to the Highway and Buildings Committee. Die Chairman : Do you agree to that Mr. Wilson. Air. Wilson: Yea. If it is understood to mean busi- ness. (Laughter). The resolution was unanimously agreed to. TECHNICAL INSTRUC HON.—INCREASES OF SALARY.—THE ART SCHOOL FEES. - -</p>
        <p>The Technical Instructioil Committee recommended that the salary of Mr. H.E. Mayston,assistant maiter,be increased from £l2O t 3 £l3O per annum. The increase to commence from the Ist September next ; that Miss Westmore, assistant mistress, be appointed teacher of French for the evening classes at a salary oi £lO for the Ses3ion for the Elementary St ige, and £lO for the Ad- vanced Stage ; that in future the fees for the Art School b 3 as follows :-33 per session for each group as defined by the Science and trt Directory, except that a separate fee of 3s be paid for clay modelling for the session and 103 6J per term for painting (three terms in the session.) Mr. J. Wilson moved the adoption of the above. Mr. Samuel Taylor seconded. Mr. D. Bisbrown demurred to the increase of salaries and considered it was time that the Council should take its stan J. Be would not question the worthiness of Mr Maystoa to the increase, hut he did not think as a Coun- cil they should be continually making these increases. The Chairman : It does not affect the rates at all. We cannot go beyond a penny rate. . .</p>
        <p>_ The proposition was carried. REPAYMENT 01,- A LOAN.</p>
        <p>The Finance Cornmitteo recommended that the Treasurer be authori;sci to realise the Council's inve.t. merit in Consols, nominal value when invested £968 2s. 6d., to enable the Council to have sufficient fusds in hand to repay the sum of £2,000 owing to the Corpora- tion of Blackburn ; also that a strong room be erected adjoining the Secretary's OtfiCP, in accordance with plans submitted, at an estimated cost of £100; and that application be made to the L)cal Govemment Board for their sanction to the borrowing of such a sum for a period of thirty years. The Finance Committee's report was adopted en the motion of Mr. E. Im•son, seconded by Mr. W. Handley.</p>
        <p>SIGNS OF PROSPERITY.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Imis)n al-so referred to the County Rate basis and ohoerved that roughly speaking the figure last year was £63.000 whereas this year £60,000 was about the mark. Thus there was a reduction of about £3,009 in the basis ot the County Rate ASPPSSMeht for the Run- corn District. It was worthy of comment that the county snowed a hea tiler and better assessment, and he tiopsd the increased surplus which the county would be able to accumulate would be devoted to furthering and assisting their education schemes. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Handley concurrcl in di, ri-marks of Mr. Irnii CHAIRMAN'S COMMUNICATIONS._</p>
        <p>The Ch:irman read a letter from the Clerk te the County Council stating that the application to have the road from the railway station to the boundary near the Hospital dec'ared a main road would have to stand over until October next as applications of this nature were only considered at an annual meeting held in that month.</p>
        <p>Letters were also read frt,m Mr. C. S. Roußdi-11, and Sir J. T. Itrunner accepting the invitation of the Council to come and deliver the prizes at the Technical Institute. The former will deliver the evening and the other the day school prizes. The date of the priza day has not yet bcen fixed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-07_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>SCHOLARSHIPS ALLOTMENTS.</head>
        <p>SCHOLARSHIPS ALLOTMENTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wilson having paid a high compliment to the success of the scholar Baguley who succeeded in gaining one of the Agricultural Certificates (see another column), proposed the following resolution " That the Technical Instruction Committee be empowered to take such steps with regard to this meeting, and to allot such scholar- ships." The resolution was framed to enable them to carry out a scheme for a competition for certain scholar- ships amongst the day scholars of the Technical Institu- tion and the passing of the resolution that day would enable them to proceed with the advertising of the arrangements and the examination of the scholars with- out delay. Mr. Handley seconded. Mr. Imison said before the resolution was put, he should like t, support all that Mr. Wilson had said, with respect to the success of the scholar Baguley. It was a scholarship which entitled the boy to higher education somewhere else, and was something that would be use- ful to him. It eatitled; him to three years' education ; food and lodgings ; and medical attendance and books wad other things would also be provided where the Parents were unabls to do so—but in this case that was not asked. The class five scholarship was one that the County Council were anxious should be more known. He thought the teachers ought to be congratulated. The Chairman considered it was not only satisfactory</p>
