        <p>o the ratepayers, but also to those who had supported he institution, arid saikfactory to everyone all round. THE WATER COMMITTEE. The Council then went into committee on the report of the Water C mmittee concerning a complaint received from the Australian Alum Company, respecting the hardness of the water supply. Later we gleaned that the Council decided to take no steps in the matter considering that the water supply was ample for both domestic and trade purpmes. RUNCORN HEATH AND COMMON. Mr. W Handley reported that Mr. James Wilding had prepared the draft plan of the scheme for making the Runcorn Heath and Common into a recreation ground and that the matter would no doubt come bet ore the next meeting of the Council. Satisfaction was expressed at the announcement. This concluded the business.</p>
        <p>DR. J. COLLIS BROWNE'S CHLORODYNE. THE ORIGINAL AND ONLY GENUINE. rfILORODYNE is admitted by the Profession to he the most wonderful and valuable remedy ever dis sovered. O.IILORODYNE is tbe hest remedy known for Coughs, Consumption, Bronchitis and Asthma. CiILORODYNE effectually checks and arrests those too often fatal diseases—Diphtheria, Fever, 23roup Ague. ofILORODYNE acts like a charm in Diarrhoea and is the only specific in Cholera and Dysentsry. CULORODYNE effectually cuts short all attacks of Epilepsy, Hysteria. Palpitation, and Spasms. 011LORODYNE is the only palliative in Neural- gia, Rheumatism, Gout, Cancer. Toothache, Meningitis, Scc. From Dr. B. J. BOULTON AND CO., Horncastle. We have made pretty extensive use of Chlorodyne inour practice lately and look upon it as an excellent direct Seda- tive and Anti-spasmodic. It seems to allay pain and irrita- tion in whatever organ, and from whatever cause. It induces a feeling of comfort and quietude not obtainable by any other remedy, and it seems to possess this great advantage over all other sedatives ; that it leaves no unpleasant after effects. " Earl Russell communicated to the College of Physicians that he received a despatch from her Majesty's Consul at Manilla to the effect that Cholera had been raging fearfully, and that the ONLY remedy of any service was CHLORO- DYNE."—See Lancet, Ist December. 1864. CAUTION.—BEWARE OF PIRACY AND IMITA- TIONS. CAUTION.—ViCe-ChAnebbor SI!. W. PAGE WOOD stated that Dr. J. COLLIS BROWSE was, anioubtedly, the inventor of " CHLORODYNE " and that the story of the defendant FREEMAN, was deliberately untrue, which he regostted to say had been sworn to.—See Times, 13th July, 1864. Sold in bottles at Is. 1it..., 2s. 9d., and 4s. 6d. None is genuine without the words "DR. J. COLLIS BROWNE'S CHLORODYJNE "on the Government Stamp. Overwhelm- ing Medics I testimony accompanies each bottle. SOLE MANUFACTURERS :—J. T. DAVENPORT, 33, Great nisch-street. Bloomsbury. :London<