        <p>LATEST NE WS- REVOLUTION IN BULGARIA. FLIGHT OF PRINCE FERDINAND. A Central News telegram from Bu la Pcsth states that a revolution is repotted to have taken place at Sophia. The revolution, it is alleged, took pace through the streets and solve of the trcops joined them. They hourly secured large numbers of adherents, and Prince Ferdin- and then fled his capital, and is now a fugitive from the country. The papers assert that all the telegraph wires were first cut to prevent any communicetion with foreign Governments. The news has created intense excitement. UNIVERSITY CRICKETERS IN THE POLICE COURT SCENE OUTSIDE A LONDON THEATRE. Gilbert Jessop, tweety.five, undergraduate, E. W. Edmundson, merchant, and F. W. Stocks, no cccupa- tion, were charged at Merlboreugh-street, London, with being disorderly. The police said there was a crowd round the defendants outside the Empire, and, as he could not induce defendants to go away, he took them into custody. They were not drunk. An atten- dant at the Empire gave evidence ai to ejecting Stocks, who, he said, was pushing people about. Mr. Fenwick said defendants had acted foolishly, but he thought the ends of justice would be served by discharging them. - SINGULAR FATALITY IN THE ISLE OF MAN. BOY DROWNED IN THE LAXEY WHEEL. An inquest was held yesterday at Laxey, concerning the death of Sydney Mark Clague, a boy whese dead body was found in the case of the celebrated great wheel at Laxey. On Tuesday night the boy while crossing a stream feeding the wheel fell from the bridge, and the body was carried by the flood into the case. A verdict of found drowned was returned. A MATRIMONIAL TRAGEDY IN INDIA. MURDER AND SUICIDE. BOMBAT, THURSDAY AFTERNOON. Mr. Gregory, a railway engineer at Igatpuri, shot his wife last night, and afterwards fired at Captain Ire- monger, of the Durham Regiment. Jealousy was the cause of the crime,Mrs. Gregory having been constantly in the company of the captain at Bandora, where the tragedy took place. The woman was killed, and Captain Iremonger is in such a precarieus condition that little hopes are entertained of his recovery. Sub- sequently Mr. Gregory committed suicide. THE FLOODS IN TEXAS. TWO HUNDRED LIVES LOST. NEW YORK, THURSDAY. A despatch from Calvert, Texai, states that fully 200 persons lost their lives in the fleoes within a radius of 30 miles from Zealia, on Tuesday night. Nearly 100 were drowned yesterday in the vicinity of the Mound, a small piece of high ground near Old San Philipi, to which hundreds of negroes had fled from the Brazes Bottoms, taking their horses and cattle with them. TRAGIC AFFAIR IN EDINBURGH. An Edinburgh correspondent wires that a sad tragedy was enacted in Edinburgh yestercley morning. A tele- phone superintendent at Malta,who is in this country on leave, and who is named Frederick Warwick, shot at his son Charles, nine years old and killed him. Warwick then shot at another son named Afred, but missed him, and then shot himself in the head and died immediately. No reason is assigned for the action. Warwick leaves a widow and two children. LATEST LOCAL WEATHER FORECAST. The following forecast of the weather for the ensuing twenty-four hours in the north-went of England was issued at the Meteorological Office at three o'clock yesterday afternoon : South-westerly breezes. Light, fair as a whole, but local showers. OSGOLDC ROSS ELECTION, RESULT OF THE POLLING. The votes recorded on Weda:sday at toe election in the Osgoldcross Division were counted yesterday, with the following results -- Sir JOHN AUSTIN (L) 5,818 CHARLES ROBER rs (L A Local Veto) 2,873 -- Majority YESTERDAY'S CRICKET, CLOSE OF PLAY. LANCASHIRE v. SOMERSET :—Lancashire all out, 206 ; Somerset, 70 for 9. GENTLEMEN v. PLAYERS :—Players, 413 for 5. Abel made 195. ' KENT v. MIDDLESEX.—Kent 132 for 6. NOTTS v. M.C.C.—Notts 133; M.C.C. 213 for 5. Wrathall net out 100. LIVERPOOL and DISTRICT v. CAMBRIDGE UNIVERSITY :—Liverpool, 212 ; Cambridge, 54 for 5</p>
